ve integers with sum \\(c\\)\u201d. This is false for any \\(c\\ge3\\).  \n  **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the claim of uniqueness on a diagonal is incorrect.\n\nBecause the foundational claim that each non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\) is false, the subsequent counting argument collapses.\n\n#### Step 2 \u2013 Counting argument using \\(s\\cdot1+(n-s)\\cdot n\\).\n\nThe solution writes:\n\\[\n\\text{total covered} \\le s\\cdot1+(n-s)\\cdot n = n^2-s(n-1).\n\\]\nThis expression assumes the (incorrect) bound \u201ceach non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point\u201d. Since that assumption is false, the inequality is unjustified. Consequently the derived inequality\n\\[\nn^2-s(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\]\nand the conclusion \\(s\\le3\\) are baseless.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the entire Lemma\u202f1 is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Claim: \u201cFor each \\(s\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\u201d\n\n#### Step 1 \u2013 Construction of a \u201csunny covering family\u201d.\n\nThe solution proposes the lines\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac12 x + \\frac{i}{2},\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\nand attempts to prove that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on some \\(S_{i}\\) by setting \\(i=a+b-1\\) and writing\n\\[\nb = \\frac12 a + \\frac{i}{2}\n\\iff 2b-a = i = a+b-1,\n\\]\nclaiming the last equality holds identically.\n\n**Analysis:** Substituting \\(i=a+b-1\\) into the left\u2011hand side gives\n\\[\n\\frac12 a + \\frac{a+b-1}{2} = \\frac{2a + b -1}{2},\n\\]\nwhich equals \\(b\\) only when \\(b = 2a-1\\). This is not true for arbitrary \\((a,b)\\) in \\(T_n\\). Hence the lines \\(S_i\\) do **not** cover all required points. The algebraic verification is incorrect, and the existence of a configuration with \\(s=0\\) (all sunny) is not established.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the proposed sunny family fails to satisfy the covering condition.\n\n#### Step 2 \u2013 \u201cReplacing the first few lines\u201d.\n\nThe argument relies on the (false) uniqueness of each \\(S_i\\) for a given diagonal. It states:\n\n* \u201c\\(S_1\\) is the unique line that contains the point \\((1,1)\\).\u201d\n* \u201c\\(S_2\\) is the unique line that contains the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).\u201d\n* \u201c\\(S_3\\) is the unique line that contains the three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\u201d\n\nThese statements are false because many lines of slope \\(\\frac12\\) (or any other slope) can pass through a given point, and a line of slope \\(\\frac12\\) cannot pass through **both** \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) simultaneously (their slopes differ). The claimed \u201cuniqueness\u201d is therefore baseless.\n\nConsequently the replacements for \\(s=1,2,3\\) (using vertical, horizontal, and diagonal lines) are built on an invalid foundation. Even if the replacements were possible, the argument does not verify that the remaining points are still covered, because the original covering family does not work.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the replacement constructions are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nBoth Lemma\u202f1 (necessary bound) and Lemma\u202f2 (sufficiency constructions) contain critical logical and factual mistakes. Therefore the derived set of possible values for \\(k\\) is not justified. The solution does not meet the standards of a rigorous IMO\u2011level proof."

