            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Covering with zero sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nAll the lines \\(L_t\\) are parallel to the forbidden diagonal, hence none is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) is a covering of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Coverings with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Base case \\(k=2\\).**  \n\nConsider the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\;y=x+1 ,\\qquad\nS_2:\\;y=-2x+n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth have admissible slopes (\\(1\\) and \\(-2\\)).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_2=\\{S_1,S_2\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{t_1,t_2\\}\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_1\\) (resp. \\(t_2\\)) is the unique index such that the point \\((1,1)\\) (resp. \\((1,n-1)\\)) lies on \\(L_{t_1}\\) (resp. \\(L_{t_2}\\)).  \nA straightforward check shows that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs either to \\(S_1\\), to \\(S_2\\) or to one of the remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines \\(L_t\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal C_2\\) is a covering with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(2\\le k<n\\)) has been constructed.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_k=\\mathcal C_k\\setminus\\{\\text{sunny lines of }\\mathcal C_k\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of non‑sunny lines; \\(|\\mathcal N_k|=n-k\\).  \nBecause each line of \\(\\mathcal N_k\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, there exists a point \\(P\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) that is covered **only** by a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal N_k\\) (otherwise the non‑sunny lines would already cover all points, contradicting the fact that at least one of them is being replaced).  \n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\lambda\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal C_k\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\lambda\\) exists because the excluded set is finite while \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite.  \nDefine the new sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y-P_y=\\lambda (x-P_x).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) passes through \\(P\\) and, by construction, is sunny and has a slope distinct from all existing sunny lines.  \n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{k+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal C_k\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S\\}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal C_{k+1}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, and still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) (the only point possibly lost after removing \\(L\\) is \\(P\\), which is now covered by \\(S\\)).  \n\nThus the induction proceeds, giving a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence of a covering with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny; each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal C\\},\\quad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal C\\},\\quad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines in }\\mathcal C\\},\n\\]\nso \\(h+v+d=n-1\\).\n\nA horizontal line can contain points with only one fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate, a vertical line only one fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate, and a diagonal line only one fixed value of \\(x+y\\).  \nConsequently the collection of all non‑sunny lines can realise at most  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct triples \\((x,y,x+y)\\).  \n\nThe sunny line \\(S\\) has a slope different from the three forbidden slopes; therefore it can introduce at most one new value for each of the three invariants, i.e. at most three new triples.  \nHence the whole family \\(\\mathcal C\\) can realise at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+3=n+2\n\\]\n\ndistinct triples \\((x,y,x+y)\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) yields a distinct triple \\((x,y,x+y)\\).  By (1),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n+2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C\\) cannot cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\), a contradiction.  \nTherefore a covering with exactly one sunny line does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nWe have exhibited coverings for \\(k=0\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\), and we have proved that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
