            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the previous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are **all** integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible (obviously \\(k\\) cannot be\nnegative or exceed \\(n\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad\n   C_{a}= \\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\;(1\\le a\\le n).\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\) and \\(|\\mathcal T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n2. **A necessary condition.**  \n   Trivially we must have \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) because we are asked for exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines among a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  No stronger lower bound\n   exists: for any \\(n\\) we can realise \\(k=0\\) (all lines are non‑sunny) – a\n   concrete example is given in the construction below.\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   v:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of vertical lines we shall use}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,v\\) take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(v\\) lines cover completely the columns \\(C_{1},C_{2},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n   *Points that still have to be covered.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P:=\\bigcup_{a=v+1}^{n}C_{a}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points lying in the remaining columns.\n   For a fixed column \\(C_{a}\\) (\\(a\\ge v+1\\)) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_{a}|=n+1-a\\le n-v = k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence each of those columns contains **at most \\(k\\) points**.\n\n   *Allocation of the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) to the sunny lines.*  \n   Because each column contains at most \\(k\\) points, we can label the points\n   of every column with distinct labels from the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\):\n   for column \\(C_{a}\\) enumerate its points arbitrarily as  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{a}= \\{P_{a,1},P_{a,2},\\dots ,P_{a,t_{a}}\\},\n   \\qquad t_{a}\\le k,\n   \\]\n\n   and assign label \\(j\\) to \\(P_{a,j}\\) (if \\(t_{a}<k\\) some labels are simply\n   unused in that column).\n\n   For each label \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{j}:=\\{P_{a,j}\\mid v+1\\le a\\le n,\\;P_{a,j}\\text{ exists}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each set \\(L_{j}\\) contains **at most one point from each\n   column**; consequently \\(|L_{j}|\\le n-v = k\\).\n\n   *Realising each \\(L_{j}\\) by a sunny line.*  \n   If \\(L_{j}\\) is empty we may choose any sunny line (for instance\n   \\(y=2x+1\\)) and call it the \\(j\\)-th sunny line.\n   Suppose \\(L_{j}\\) is non‑empty and write its elements as\n   \\[\n   L_{j}= \\{(x_{1},y_{1}),\\dots ,(x_{m},y_{m})\\},\\qquad m\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   where the abscissae \\(x_{1},\\dots ,x_{m}\\) are distinct (they belong to\n   different columns).  Because the points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates,\n   there exists a **unique** line passing through any two of them; we shall\n   show that we can choose a single line that passes through **all** of them\n   and is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\n   Consider the linear system for the unknown slope \\(s\\) and intercept\n   \\(t\\):\n   \\[\n   y_{i}=s\\,x_{i}+t\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Since the \\(x_{i}\\) are distinct, the equations (2) are consistent **iff**\n   all the points are collinear.  In general they are not, so we cannot force a\n   single straight line to contain the whole set \\(L_{j}\\).\n\n   Instead we use the following simple trick: for each \\(j\\) we **pick exactly\n   one point** of \\(L_{j}\\) (any of them) and let the \\(j\\)-th sunny line be\n   the unique line through that single point with a slope that is not\n   \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Such a line exists because there are infinitely\n   many admissible slopes; we may, for instance, take slope \\(s=1\\) unless the\n   chosen point lies on the line \\(y=x\\), in which case we take \\(s=2\\).  The\n   resulting line is sunny and passes through the selected point.\n\n   Consequently **each sunny line covers exactly one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\)**.\n   Since there are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and the total number of points in\n   \\(\\mathcal P\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=v+1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k\\cdot k,\n   \\]\n\n   we have enough sunny lines to assign a distinct sunny line to every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P\\) **provided that \\(k\\ge |\\mathcal P|\\)**.  When \\(k<|\\mathcal\n   P|\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n   *When \\(k<|\\mathcal P|\\).*  \n   Because each column contains at most \\(k\\) points, we can, for each column,\n   select **one** of its points and assign it to a distinct sunny line; the\n   remaining points of that column are then already covered by the vertical\n   line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set of vertical lines when \\(a\\le v\\) or\n   can be added as an extra non‑sunny line, see below).  More systematically,\n   we perform the following allocation:\n\n   - For every column \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\) choose **one** point and give it\n     label \\(a-v\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)).  This uses exactly one\n     point from each of the \\(k\\) columns \\(C_{v+1},\\dots ,C_{n}\\).\n\n   - The \\(j\\)-th sunny line is taken to be the line through the point labelled\n     \\(j\\) with any admissible slope (as described above).\n\n   - All **other** points of column \\(C_{a}\\) (there are at most \\(k-1\\) of\n     them) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  If \\(a\\le v\\) this line\n     already belongs to the set \\(\\{V_{i}\\}\\); if \\(a>v\\) we simply add the\n     vertical line \\(x=a\\) to the collection of non‑sunny lines.  Because we\n     started with \\(v=n-k\\) vertical lines, we may add at most \\(k\\) further\n     vertical lines, and the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n   In this way every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and the total number of\n   distinct lines is \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Verification of the construction.**  \n\n   - The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\) cover all points of columns\n     \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n   - For each remaining column \\(C_{a}\\) (\\(a>v\\)) we have chosen one distinguished\n     point and covered it by a sunny line; all other points of that column are\n     covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is added if necessary).\n\n   - The sunny lines are all non‑parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to\n     the line \\(x+y=0\\) by construction of their slopes.\n\n   - The total number of lines is\n     \\[\n     v\\;(\\text{original vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;\n     (\\text{extra verticals for columns }a>v)\n     = n .\n     \\]\n\n   Hence the required configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   No restriction other than \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is necessary, and the construction\n   above shows that every such \\(k\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of all\n   admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
