            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty construction by a rigorous one.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are **all** integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible (obviously \\(k\\) cannot be negative or exceed \\(n\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad\n   C_{a}= \\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\;(1\\le a\\le n).\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\) and \\(|\\mathcal T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n2. **Trivial bound.**  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines must satisfy  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   v:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny vertical lines we shall use}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical (non‑sunny) lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,v\\) take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(v\\) lines cover completely the columns \\(C_{1},C_{2},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n   *Points that still have to be covered.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P:=\\bigcup_{a=v+1}^{n}C_{a}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points lying in the remaining columns.  \n   For a fixed column \\(C_{a}\\) (\\(a\\ge v+1\\)) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_{a}|=n+1-a\\le n-v = k .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence each of those columns contains **at most \\(k\\) points**.\n\n   *Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{j}:\\;y = x-(v+j-1) .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_{j}\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore none of them is parallel\n   to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Consequently each\n   \\(S_{j}\\) is a sunny line.\n\n   *Verification that the sunny lines cover \\(\\mathcal P\\).*  \n   Take a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).  By definition \\(a\\ge v+1\\) and\n   \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Compute  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b = a-(a-(v+j-1)) = v+j-1 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   b = a-(v+j-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le b = a-(v+j-1)\\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   The left inequality gives \\(j\\le a-v\\); the right inequality gives\n   \\(j\\ge a-v\\).  Hence \\(j = a-v\\).  Since \\(a\\) ranges from \\(v+1\\) to \\(n\\),\n   the integer \\(j=a-v\\) indeed belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Therefore the\n   point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (6) with this value of \\(j\\) and lies on\n   the sunny line \\(S_{j}\\).\n\n   Consequently **every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on exactly one of the\n   sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\).**\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The total collection of lines consists of  \n\n   * the \\(v=n-k\\) vertical lines \\((2)\\);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\((5)\\).\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   v+k = (n-k)+k = n .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope,\n   whereas the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the two required conditions.**  \n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) belongs either to a column\n   \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\le v\\) (hence is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\)),\n   or to a column \\(C_{a}\\) with \\(a\\ge v+1\\) (hence is covered by the sunny\n   line \\(S_{a-v}\\) as proved in step 3).  Thus each point of the set\n   \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny; the remaining\n   \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical and therefore non‑sunny.  Hence the configuration\n   contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above yields\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions of the problem, and no\n   other value of \\(k\\) is possible because of the obvious bound (1).  Hence the\n   set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
