ts \\(c=i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   The set \\(D_k\\) contains exactly \\(n-k\\) lines; each has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is not sunny.\n\n3. **The family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k = \\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup D_k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The sunny lines have pairwise different slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) and none of these slopes equals \\(-1\\); consequently no \\(s_i\\) coincides with any diagonal line. The diagonal lines in \\(D_k\\) are distinct because they have different constants \\(c\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Covering property.**  \n   The points \\((i,1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(s_i\\).  \n   Any other point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has sum \\(c=a+b\\) with \\(c\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(d_c\\), which belongs to \\(D_k\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nBecause the problem fixes the total number of lines to be \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. The construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Hence these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
