e point \\((p,1)\\) leads to the same contradiction.\n\n   **Case 3.** \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains at least two distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\), say \\(x+y=r\\) and \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(r<s\\).  \n   Consider the point \\((1,r-1)\\) (which satisfies \\(1+(r-1)=r\\le n+1\\) because \\(r\\le n\\)).  \n   This point can be covered only by a line of the form \\(x+y=r\\), a horizontal line \\(y=r-1\\), or a vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   As before, at most one of the latter two families can provide a line (otherwise we would have two lines of the same forbidden direction).  \n   Consequently the point cannot be covered unless the unique sunny line coincides with one of the admissible lines, which again leaves another admissible line uncovered.  \n\n   In all three cases we obtain a contradiction, therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n   Hence \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\), for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\), and for \\(k=n\\), and the impossibility of \\(k=1\\), we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nAll other lines are called **non‑sunny**.\n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. for every pair of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions giving admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; \\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i=1\\) the two points coincide, and we interpret \\(L_{1}\\) as the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nFor \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{1-i}{i-1}= -\\frac{1}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSet \\(t=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\); then \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a}\\) because both points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,a)\\) belong to that line; similarly if \\(b>a\\) the point lies on \\(L_{b}\\).  Consequently every required point is covered and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n#### 1.3  The case \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(s\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\) and set \\(k=s\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  Use the \\(s\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{s}\\) defined in 1.2.\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  For each \\(j\\in\\{s+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(H_{j}\\) are non‑sunny and distinct because the constants \\(j\\) are distinct.\n\nNow consider a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le s\\)*, then \\(\\max\\{a,b\\}\\le s\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\max\\{a,b\\}}\\) (the same argument as in 1.2).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge s+1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  Hence the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n\nThus every required point is covered, and exactly the \\(s\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{s}\\) are sunny.  Consequently every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly one sunny line, which we denote by \\(S\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  Partition the non‑sunny lines into three families:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{ \\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{ \\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{ \\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause there are \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) non‑sunny lines, at least one of the families contains at least two distinct lines.  We treat the three possibilities separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Two horizontal lines\n\nSuppose \\(\\mathcal H\\) contains distinct lines \\(y=p\\) and \\(y=q\\) with \\(p<q\\).  \nSince the covering condition only concerns points with second coordinate at most \\(n\\), we have \\(q\\le n\\).  \nConsider the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,q).\n\\]\n\nWe have \\(1+q\\le n+1\\), so \\(P\\) must belong to some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nA line that passes through \\(P\\) can be of three possible types:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=q\\) (which is already present in \\(\\mathcal H\\));\n* a vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. of the form \\(x+y=r\\) with \\(r=1+q\\).\n\nIf the sunny line \\(S\\) is neither \\(x=1\\) nor the line \\(x+y=1+q\\), then \\(P\\) is not covered—a contradiction.  \n\nIf \\(S\\) equals one of those two lines, then the other one is still missing, because we have at most one line of each forbidden direction (otherwise two lines would belong to the same family, contradicting the assumption that only \\(\\mathcal H\\) has multiplicity \\(\\ge2\\)).  Hence \\(P\\) is again uncovered.  This contradiction shows that the case of two horizontal lines cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Two vertical lines\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  Suppose \\(\\mathcal V\\) contains \\(x=p\\) and \\(x=q\\) with \\(p<q\\) (both \\(\\le n\\)).  \nThe point \\(Q=(p,1)\\) satisfies \\(p+1\\le n+1\\) and can be covered only by the vertical line \\(x=p\\), a horizontal line \\(y=1\\), or a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=p+1\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as in 2.1 yields a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Two lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains distinct lines \\(x+y=r\\) and \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(r<s\\) (both \\(\\le n+1\\)).  \nTake the point  \n\n\\[\nR=(1,r-1).\n\\]\n\nWe have \\(1+(r-1)=r\\le n+1\\), so \\(R\\) must be covered.  \nA line through \\(R\\) can be\n\n* the line \\(x+y=r\\) (already in \\(\\mathcal D\\));\n* the horizontal line \\(y=r-1\\);\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nAgain, if the sunny line \\(S\\) is not one of the latter two, \\(R\\) is uncovered.  \nIf \\(S\\) equals, say, \\(y=r-1\\), then the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is still missing, because we have at most one line of each forbidden direction (otherwise two vertical lines would belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\), contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(\\mathcal D\\) has multiplicity \\(\\ge2\\)).  Hence \\(R\\) cannot be covered, a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince all three possibilities lead to contradictions, the assumption that a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists is false.  Therefore **\\(k=1\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\);\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\);\n* \\(k=n\\),\n\nand we have proved that no configuration can have exactly one sunny line.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
