(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R\\) be **irrational**. Define  \n\n\\[\ns:\\;y=\\alpha x+\\beta,\\qquad\\beta:=q-\\alpha p .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n1. \\(s\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and it is not vertical);\n2. \\((p,q)\\in s\\);\n3. No other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n(1) and (2) follow directly from the definition of \\(\\beta\\) and the fact that an irrational number is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor (3), suppose that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(p,q)\\) also satisfies \\(b=\\alpha a+\\beta\\). Substituting \\(\\beta\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-q=\\alpha (a-p). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\neq p\\) then \\(\\displaystyle \\alpha=\\frac{b-q}{a-p}\\). The right‑hand side is a ratio of two integers, hence rational. This contradicts the irrationality of \\(\\alpha\\). Therefore we must have \\(a=p\\), and then (1) forces \\(b=q\\), contradicting \\((a,b)\\neq(p,q)\\). Hence no other lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(s\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct irrational numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that none equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=\\sqrt{2}+i\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\;y=\\alpha_i x+(1-\\alpha_i i).\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each \\(s_i\\) is sunny and contains **exactly** the point \\((i,1)\\) of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Remaining diagonal lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_k=\\bigl\\{d_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we keep all diagonal lines except those with constants \\(c=i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   The set \\(D_k\\) contains exactly \\(n-k\\) lines; each has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is not sunny.\n\n3. **The family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup D_k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The sunny lines have pairwise different irrational slopes, none of which equals \\(-1\\); consequently no \\(s_i\\) coincides with any diagonal line. The diagonal lines in \\(D_k\\) are distinct because they have different constants \\(c\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Covering property.**  \n   - The points \\((i,1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(s_i\\).  \n   - Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) be any other point. Its sum \\(c=a+b\\) satisfies \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) but \\(c\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) (otherwise \\((a,b)=(i,1)\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\)). Hence the diagonal line \\(d_c\\) belongs to \\(D_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nBecause the problem fixes the total number of lines to be \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. The construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Consequently these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
