"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified a flaw in the previous construction: for \\(k\\ge 2\\) some points of the required set are not covered. After a careful analysis I conclude that the only possible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Baseline covering (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(d_c:\\;x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny (each has slope \\(-1\\)), and together they contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n2. **A sunny line cannot replace a diagonal (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  \n   *If \\(L\\) has rational slope* \\(p/q\\) (in lowest terms) then any two distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) differ by a vector \\((q, p)\\). Because all points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most one such vector can fit inside the triangle, so \\(L\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *If \\(L\\) has irrational slope* then it contains at most one lattice point at all.  \n\n   Consequently **every sunny line contains at most one point of the set**\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Counting argument (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   The set \\(T_n\\) has \\(\\displaystyle |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \n   Each non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If we use \\(k\\) sunny lines, they together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (by Lemma\u202f2).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in C}(c-1)\\le\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   points, where \\(C\\) is any subset of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(n-k\\).  \n   Equality in the above sum occurs only when **all** diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) are present, i.e. when \\(k=0\\).  \n   For any \\(k\\ge1\\) we must omit at least one diagonal, thereby losing at least one point of \\(T_n\\); the sunny lines can recover at most one point, so the total number of covered points is strictly less than \\(|T_n|\\). Hence the covering condition cannot be satisfied when \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must decide for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* the lines \\(\\{d_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct;\n* each \\(d_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\), hence is **not** sunny;\n*  \n\n  \\[\n  T_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}\\bigl(d_c\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n  \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in d_c\\). Conversely, any point of \\(d_c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) automatically satisfies \\(a+b=c\\le n+1\\), hence belongs to \\(T_n\\). Distinct constants give distinct lines, and all have slope \\(-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A sunny line meets at most one point of \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.\n\n*If the slope of \\(L\\) is irrational*, then \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice point, in particular at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If the slope of \\(L\\) is rational*,