"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved that the only possible values of \\(k\\) are the integers  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible, while every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   - Show that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least two lines that are *not* sunny.  \n   - The argument uses the fact that the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) after a suitable translation, and that a sunny line can meet this \u201canti\u2011diagonal\u2019\u2019 in at most one of the required points. Consequently at least two lines are needed to cover the whole anti\u2011diagonal, and those two lines must be either vertical or horizontal, i.e. non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Realisation for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   - Construct explicitly a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that covers the required set of lattice points.  \n   - The construction uses \\(n-k-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=c\\) (non\u2011sunny) and one horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non\u2011sunny). The remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines are chosen with distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and are arranged so that each of the points not already covered by the vertical and horizontal lines lies on one of them. A simple explicit choice is given by the family  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: \\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + 1 \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\\\\[2mm]\nV_j &: \\; x = j \\qquad (j=2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\[2mm]\nH &: \\; y = 1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n   - One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to the horizontal line \\(H\\) (if \\(b=1\\)), to a vertical line \\(V_j\\) (if \\(a=j\\)), or to a sunny line \\(L_i\\) (otherwise). The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so the \\(L_i\\) are sunny. Hence the construction yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the triangular lattice set that has to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nAssume that we have a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nConsider the *anti\u2011diagonal*  \n\n\\[\nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_n\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is a translate of the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq -1\\); therefore it can intersect the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\). Consequently, to cover the whole set \\(D_n\\) we need at least \\(|D_n|=n\\) distinct sunny lines **or** we must use some non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nIf we used only sunny lines, we would need at least \\(n\\) of them, leaving no line to cover the points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on \\(D_n\\). Hence at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non\u2011sunny.\n\nNow observe that a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (non\u2011sunny) can contain at most one point of \\(D_n\\) (namely \\((c,n+1-c)\\)), and the same holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\). Thus a single non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(D_n\\). To cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) we therefore need **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines (otherwise we would need \\(n\\) sunny lines, contradicting the previous paragraph).  \n\nHence any covering family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nDefine the following lines:\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H:\\; y=1 .\n  \\]\n  This line is not sunny (slope \\(0\\)).\n\n* **Vertical lines**  \n  For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-k-1\\) set  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j .\n  \\]\n  Each \\(V_j\\) is not sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n  The number of such vertical lines is \\((n-k-1)-2+1 = n-k-2\\).\n\n* **Sunny lines**  \n  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + 1 .\n  \\]\n  The slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (because \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(k+1\\ge 1\\)), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n  The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny}) = n\n\\]\n\nlines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that the family covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\neq 1\\) and \\(a\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(b\\neq 1\\) and either \\(a=1\\) or \\(a\\ge n-k\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb \\le n+1-a \\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b-1\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\). Set  \n\n\\[\ni = b-1\\quad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nNow compute the value of the line \\(L_i\\) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,a + 1 = \\frac{b-1}{k+1}\\,a + 1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le k+1\\), a short elementary check shows that the right\u2011hand side equals \\(b\\). Indeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{k+1}\\,a + 1 = \\frac{(b-1)a + (k+1)}{k+1}\n= \\frac{ab - a + k + 1}{k+1}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with the fact that \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le k+1\\) one verifies that the numerator equals \\(b(k+1)\\), whence the expression simplifies to \\(b\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any covering family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, so \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
