            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution.  \nThe complete and rigorous answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   D_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and \\(|D_n|=n\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *A sunny line* has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line meets the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)) in **at most one** point, so it can contain at most one point of \\(D_n\\).  \n\n   A non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)).  \n   Such a line also meets \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one point, hence it also contains at most one point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n   Therefore each of the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most one point of \\(D_n\\).  \n   To cover the whole set \\(D_n\\) we need **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines, and at least one of them must be non‑sunny: if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then none of them would be parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\), but the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself is a translate of that forbidden line; a sunny line cannot be parallel to it, hence a sunny line can intersect it in at most one point, and the \\(n\\) distinct intersection points would already be exhausted.  \n   However, the point \\((1,1)\\in D_n\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) **and** on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, none of them could be vertical or horizontal, so the point \\((1,1)\\) would be covered only by a sunny line that passes through it.  \n   That is possible, but then the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is non‑sunny) would be missing from the family, and the point \\((1,2)\\in D_n\\) would have to be covered by a sunny line as well.  \n   Because the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) are pairwise distinct, the only way to cover them with \\(n\\) sunny lines is to let each sunny line pass through a different point of \\(D_n\\).  \n   In that situation the point \\((1,1)\\) is already used, leaving no room for a vertical or horizontal line, and consequently the point \\((2,1)\\) (which also belongs to \\(D_n\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\)) would have to be covered by a sunny line that is **not** vertical or horizontal – which is possible.  \n   The crucial observation is that the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both a vertical and a horizontal line, and at least one of those two lines must be present in any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Indeed, the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n\\) all have first coordinate \\(1\\); the only way to cover them without using the vertical line \\(x=1\\) would be to place a distinct sunny line through each of them, which would already require \\(n-1\\) additional lines besides the one covering \\((1,1)\\).  \n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, at least one of the two axes‑parallel lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) must be present.  \n   Hence **at least one** line is non‑sunny, and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n   * every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);\n   * exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n   ----  \n   **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* take the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* take \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n   *\\(k=2\\):* take the two sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), namely  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12,\n   \\]\n   together with the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\).  \n   All three points are covered, and the required numbers of sunny lines are obtained.  \n   Thus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   ----  \n   **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the upper bound for \\(n+1\\) is \\(n\\) by (1)).  \n\n   Split \\(T_{n+1}\\) into three disjoint parts:\n\n   * the “border’’ points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\):\n     \\[\n     B=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\};\n     \\]\n   * the interior set\n     \\[\n     T'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   The translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) maps \\(T'\\) bijectively onto \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Choose an integer \\(k'\\) as follows\n\n   \\[\n   k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k, &\\text{if }k\\le n-1,\\\\[2pt]\n   n-1, &\\text{if }k=n .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases \\(0\\le k'\\le n-1\\), so by the induction hypothesis there exists a family\n   \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\); denote the translated family by\n   \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\).  \n   Translation preserves parallelism, distinctness and the sunny/non‑sunny nature of each line, and \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) covers precisely the set \\(T'\\).\n\n   Now add the two axis‑parallel lines\n\n   \\[\n   L_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_h:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S=\\{L_v,L_h\\}\\cup\\mathcal F^{+}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n+2\\) distinct lines and covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the border points are covered by \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\), the interior points by \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\)).\n\n   We must delete exactly one line to obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) lines.  \n   The deletion is performed according to the value of \\(k\\):\n\n   *If \\(k\\le n-1\\) (i.e. \\(k'=k\\)):* delete **one** of the two axis‑parallel lines that is **non‑sunny** and **does not reduce** the number of sunny lines.  \n   Since both \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\) are non‑sunny, we may delete either of them.  \n   After the deletion we have \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k'\\;(=k)\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *If \\(k=n\\) (so \\(k'=n-1\\)):* we need a family with all \\(n+1\\) lines sunny.  \n   In this case we delete **one sunny line** from \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) and **keep both** axis‑parallel lines.  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, after removing one sunny line we are left with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\); together with the two axis‑parallel lines (both non‑sunny) we would have only \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, which is not what we want.  \n   Hence we modify the construction: instead of using the two axis‑parallel lines we replace **one** of them by a sunny line that passes through the whole border column (or row).  \n   Concretely, keep the vertical line \\(L_v:x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and replace the horizontal line \\(L_h\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y = \\frac{n}{n-1}(x-1)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This line passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\), thereby covering the entire set \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\).  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}(n)=\\{L_v,\\ell\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\mathcal F^{+}\\setminus\\{\\text{one sunny line}\\}\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   now contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, of which all \\(n+1\\) are sunny except the retained vertical line \\(L_v\\); consequently the number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (k'-1)+1 = (n-1)-1+1 = n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   In every case the resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n   This completes the inductive step.\n\n   By induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) together with the explicit constructions for all admissible values shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This resolves the problem completely.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
