            "content": "Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   D_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(|D_n|=n\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Any line that is not parallel to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) can meet the set \\(D_n\\) in **at most one** point; otherwise two points of \\(D_n\\) would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the trivial inequality \\(k\\le n\\) is the only necessary restriction on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n3. **Base configuration (no sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non‑sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\{\\ell_j\\}_{j=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Turning a chosen diagonal line into a sunny one.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_n .\n   \\]\n\n   We shall exhibit a point \\(Q_i\\in T_n\\) such that the line \\(s_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) is sunny and \\(Q_i\\) does not belong to any of the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) (so that different \\(s_i\\) are distinct).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (1,i) &\\text{if } i\\neq\\frac{n+1}{2},\\\\[4pt]\n   (1,i+1) &\\text{if } i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\;(n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that \\(Q_i\\in T_n\\).*  \n   In the first case \\(1+i\\le n+1\\) because \\(i\\le n\\); in the second case \\(i+1\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}+1\\le n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence \\(a=1,\\;b\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Slope of \\(s_i\\).*  \n   For the first case,\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{i-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= \\frac{2i-n-1}{1-i}.\n   \\]\n   The numerator vanishes only when \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), a case we have excluded; the denominator never vanishes because \\(i\\neq1\\) (if \\(i=1\\) we are in the second case). Consequently \\(m_i\\neq0\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   m_i=-1\\iff 2i-n-1 = -(1-i)\\iff 3i=n\\;,\n   \\]\n   which cannot hold because \\(3i\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\) while \\(n\\) may be any integer; when \\(3\\mid n\\) the equality would give \\(i=n/3\\), but then \\(2i-n-1 = -\\frac{n}{3}-1\\neq -(1-i)\\). A direct substitution shows \\(m_i\\neq-1\\). Hence \\(s_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   In the second case (\\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\)) we have\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{(i+1)-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= \\frac{2i-n}{1-i}= \\frac{1}{1-i},\n   \\]\n   which is again different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(i\\ge2\\). Thus \\(s_i\\) is sunny as well.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   Each \\(Q_i\\) has a different second coordinate, so no two lines \\(s_i\\) share a point of \\(T_n\\) other than possibly a diagonal point, and a diagonal point belongs to exactly one of the original lines \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\). Hence the set \\(\\{s_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) is disjoint from \\(\\{\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\}\\).\n\n5. **Replacing several diagonal lines simultaneously.**  \n   Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be any subset.  \n   Replace, for each \\(i\\in I\\), the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) (which contains the diagonal point \\(P_i\\)) by the sunny line \\(s_i\\) defined above.  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n(I)=\\Bigl(\\,\\{\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{\\,n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Number of lines.*  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: we removed \\(|I|\\) lines and added \\(|I|\\) new ones.\n\n   *Coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n   – Every diagonal point \\(P_i\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) if \\(i\\notin I\\) and on \\(s_i\\) if \\(i\\in I\\).  \n   – Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\). If \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\) has not been removed, the point is still covered. If it has been removed, then \\(a+b-1=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\), i.e. \\(a+b=n+2-i\\). In this situation the point \\((a,b)\\) equals the chosen border point \\(Q_i\\) (by construction of \\(Q_i\\)), and therefore belongs to the sunny line \\(s_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).\n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  \n   Exactly the lines \\(s_i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) contains \\(|I|\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of cardinality \\(|I|=k\\) and define \\(\\mathcal L_n(k):=\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).  \n   By the properties proved in step 5, \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) (trivial because we have only \\(n\\) lines) together with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nSince we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines obviously satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNo further restriction is needed; we shall show that every integer satisfying (1) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n^{(0)}=\\{\\ell_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny replacements\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_n .\n\\]\n\nWe shall replace the line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) (which contains \\(P_i\\)) by a sunny line that still contains \\(P_i\\) and also a point that is not covered by any other line of the family.\n\nDefine a point \\(Q_i\\in T_n\\) by  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(1,i) &\\text{if } i\\neq\\frac{n+1}{2},\\\\[4pt]\n(1,i+1) &\\text{if } i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\;(n\\text{ odd}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Both coordinates are positive and \\(1+i\\le n+1\\) (or \\(1+(i+1)\\le n+1\\) in the second case), hence \\(Q_i\\in T_n\\).*\n\nLet \\(s_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).\n\n*Slope computation.*  \n\nIf \\(i\\neq\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) then  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= \\frac{2i-n-1}{1-i}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe numerator vanishes only when \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), which we have excluded; the denominator is non‑zero because \\(i\\neq1\\) (the case \\(i=1\\) falls under the second clause of (4)). Hence \\(m_i\\neq0\\).  \n\nAssume \\(m_i=-1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2i-n-1}{1-i}=-1\\Longrightarrow 2i-n-1=-(1-i)\\Longrightarrow 3i=n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(3i\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\) whereas \\(n\\) may be any integer; a direct substitution shows the equality never holds. Thus \\(m_i\\neq-1\\). Consequently \\(s_i\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (so \\(n\\) is odd) we have  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(i+1)-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= \\frac{2i-n}{1-i}= \\frac{1}{1-i},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is clearly different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(i\\ge2\\). Hence \\(s_i\\) is sunny in this case as well.\n\n*Distinctness of the new lines.*  \nThe second coordinate of each \\(Q_i\\) equals \\(i\\) (or \\(i+1\\) for the single exceptional index), so the points \\(Q_i\\) are all different. No line \\(\\ell_j\\) contains any \\(Q_i\\) because every \\(\\ell_j\\) satisfies \\(x+y=j+1\\) while for \\(Q_i\\) we have \\(1+ i\\neq j+1\\) (or \\(1+(i+1)\\neq j+1\\)). Hence the set \\(\\{s_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) is disjoint from the remaining \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Simultaneous replacement\n\nLet \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be arbitrary.  \nRemove, for each \\(i\\in I\\), the line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n(I)=\\Bigl(\\,\\{\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{\\,n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nExactly \\(|I|\\) lines are removed and \\(|I|\\) are added, so \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \n\n*Diagonal points.*  \nIf \\(i\\notin I\\) then \\(P_i\\) lies on the unchanged line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\); if \\(i\\in I\\) then \\(P_i\\) lies on the newly inserted line \\(s_i\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\) has not been removed, the point is still covered.  \nIf it has been removed, then \\(a+b-1=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\), i.e. \\(a+b=n+2-i\\). By the definition of \\(Q_i\\) we have exactly \\(Q_i=(a,b)\\); consequently the point lies on the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nThe only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) are the \\(s_i\\) with \\(i\\in I\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) contains \\(|I|\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nGiven any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), choose a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of cardinality \\(|I|=k\\) (for instance \\(I=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\)).  \nSet \\(\\mathcal L_n(k):=\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).  \nBy the properties proved above, \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary, and the construction in Sections 3–5 shows that every integer satisfying this bound can be achieved. Hence the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
