hich contains \\(P_i\\)) by a sunny line that still contains \\(P_i\\) and also a point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on any other line of the family.  \n\n   *Construction of the replacement line \\(s_i\\).*  \n\n   *Case A: \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Set  \n   \\[\n   Q_i=(i-1,\\;n-1-i)\\in T_n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)-(n-1-i)}{i-(i-1)}=2 .\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line\n   \\[\n   s_i:=\\overline{P_iQ_i}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   is sunny.\n\n   *Case B: \\(i=n-1\\).*  \n   Put  \n   \\[\n   Q_{n-1}=(1,n-1)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n   The slope of the line through \\(P_{n-1}=(n-1,2)\\) and \\(Q_{n-1}\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_{n-1}= \\frac{2-(n-1)}{\\,n-1-1\\,}= -\\frac{n-3}{n-2},\n   \\]\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence the line\n   \\[\n   s_{n-1}:=\\overline{P_{n-1}Q_{n-1}}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   is sunny.\n\n   *Case C: \\(i=n\\).*  \n   Put  \n   \\[\n   Q_{n}=(1,n)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(P_{n}=(n,1)\\) and \\(Q_{n}\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   m_{n}= \\frac{1-n}{\\,n-1\\,}= -\\frac{n-1}{n-1}= -1,\n   \\]\n   which is forbidden.  \n   To avoid this we choose a different interior point:\n   \\[\n   Q_{n}=(2,n-1)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\overline{P_{n}Q_{n}}\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_{n}= \\frac{n-1-1}{\\,2-n\\,}= \\frac{n-2}{2-n}= -1,\n   \\]\n   still \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence we use a third alternative: take the point\n   \\[\n   Q_{n}=(1,n-2)\\in T_n\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n   \\]\n   The slope of the line through \\(P_{n}=(n,1)\\) and this point is\n   \\[\n   m_{n}= \\frac{1-(n-2)}{\\,n-1\\,}= -\\frac{n-3}{n-1},\n   \\]\n   which is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). For the smallest admissible \\(n=3\\) we treat \\(i=n\\) separately (see below). Thus for all \\(n\\ge4\\) the line\n   \\[\n   s_{n}:=\\overline{P_{n}Q_{n}}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   is sunny.\n\n   *Special case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Here \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) and the diagonal points are\n   \\(P_1=(1,3),P_2=(2,2),P_3=(3,1)\\).  \n   The base family (1) consists of the three lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\).  \n   Replacing any of them by the line through its diagonal point and the unique remaining point of \\(T_3\\) yields a sunny line (its slope is \\(2\\) or \\(\\tfrac12\\)). Hence the construction works for \\(n=3\\) as well.\n\n   In every case the point \\(Q_i\\) lies in \\(T_n\\) and is **not** on any line \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\neq n+1-i\\); consequently the new lines \\(s_i\\) are all distinct from each other and from the unchanged \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n5. **Simultaneous replacement.**  \n   Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be any subset.  \n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) remove the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(s_i\\) constructed above.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n(I)=\\Bigl(\\,\\{\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{\\,n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Number of lines.*  \n   Exactly \\(|I|\\) lines are removed and \\(|I|\\) are added, so \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n\n   - **Diagonal points.**  \n     If \\(i\\notin I\\) then \\(P_i\\) remains on the unchanged line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\); if \\(i\\in I\\) then \\(P_i\\) lies on the newly inserted sunny line \\(s_i\\).\n\n   - **Points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).**  \n     Any such point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\).  \n     If \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\) has not been removed, the point is still covered.  \n     If it has been removed, then \\(a+b-1=n+1-i\\) for a unique \\(i\\in I\\); equivalently \\(a+b=n+2-i\\). By the explicit choice of \\(Q_i\\) (see (2)–(5)) we have exactly \\((a,b)=Q_i\\). Hence the point belongs to the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \n\n     Consequently **every** element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).\n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  \n   The only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) are the \\(s_i\\) with \\(i\\in I\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) contains \\(|I|\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of cardinality \\(|I|=k\\) (for instance \\(I=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\)).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) constructed in (6) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, because the explicit construction above works for every such \\(k\\). Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
