atisfy \\(a+c\\le n+1\\).  \n\nConsequently the four non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n4\\cdot 1 =4\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nAll remaining \\(n-4\\) lines are sunny, hence each of them can contain at most one point of \\(T_{n}\\) with a given abscissa (otherwise two points on the same sunny line would have the same slope, contradicting the definition of a sunny line).  \nThus the total number of points that can be covered by any family of \\(n\\) lines containing four non\u2011sunny members is at most  \n\n\\[\n4 + (n-4)=n .\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we need to cover \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) points for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence a configuration satisfying the covering condition cannot contain four or more non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most three, i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\le 3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(n-3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWe first build a configuration with exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines (hence three non\u2011sunny lines).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nL_{x}\\colon y=0,\\qquad L_{y}\\colon x=0,\\qquad L_{d}\\colon x+y=0\n\\]\n\nbe the three non\u2011sunny lines.  \nFor each integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n-3\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}\\colon y-1=m\\,(x-1) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (3) pass through the point \\((1,1)\\) and have distinct slopes \\(m\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); hence they are sunny and pairwise distinct.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{L_{x},L_{y},L_{d}\\}\\cup\\{S_{m}\\mid 1\\le m\\le n-3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which \\(n-3\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=0\\) or \\(y=0\\) respectively, i.e. on \\(L_{y}\\) or \\(L_{x}\\).\n* If \\(a=b\