ll \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(n-3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k<n-3\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k<n-3\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen the number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-k>3\\), contradicting the bound proved in \u00a71.  \nHence such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nn-3\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid n-3\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
