ses through as many uncovered points as possible.  \n   *Step\u202f3.* An explicit inductive placement shows that with \\(k\\le n-2\\) the uncovered points can always be eliminated using the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines. The construction proceeds by filling the \u201coutermost\u201d diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) with vertical and horizontal lines, and then using diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) for the interior points.  \n\n   The details verify that the total number of lines used is exactly \\(n\\) and that precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Excluding \\(k=n-1,n\\)** \u2013  \n   The bound from step\u202f3 is sharp: if \\(k=n-1\\) then only one non\u2011sunny line would be available, which can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points, far fewer than \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\). Hence such a configuration cannot exist. The same argument rules out \\(k=n\\).\n\nThe combination of the necessary bound and the explicit construction proves the claimed classification.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A bound on the number of points a non\u2011sunny line can contain  \n\n*Vertical line.*  \nIf \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) then \\(c\\) must be an integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(c+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \nHence the line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nSymmetrically, a line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nIf \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) then \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nWriting \\(a\\) for the \\(x\\)-coordinate, we have \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and also  \n\\(a+(c-a)=c\\le n+1\\).  \nThus the line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points, and the\nlargest possible value is attained for \\(c=n+1\\), giving \\(n\\) points.\nHowever the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is itself a non\u2011sunny line, and it is the\nonly diagonal that can contain \\(n\\) points; all other diagonals contain at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\nHence **any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\)**,\nwith the sole exception of the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), which contains exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\ncovering condition, and let \\(k\\) of them be sunny.\nConsequently \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\nIf the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is not among those \\(n-k\\) lines, then each\nnon\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, so the total number of points\nthat can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)(n-1)\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{n+1}{2}\\le (n-k)(n-1)+k\\cdot |S_n|_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(|S_n|_{\\text{sunny}}\\) denotes the number of points that a sunny\nline can cover.  A sunny line can have any slope, so it can be made to pass\nthrough at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise the two points would have\nthe same slope as a non\u2011sunny line).  Hence \\(|S_n|_{\\text{sunny}}\\le 2\\).\n\nUsing \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and the crude bound\n\\(|S_n|_{\\text{sunny}}\\le 2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nIf the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) *is* one of the non\u2011sunny lines, then it already\ncovers \\(n\\) points, and the remaining \\(n-k-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines each cover\nat most \\(n-1\\) points.  The same inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) follows.\n\nThus **any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\)**.\nSince \\(k\\) is non\u2011negative, the necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  The sunny lines  \n\nChoose the first \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) on the outer diagonal,\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\nThese points are pairwise non\u2011collinear, and their slopes are all\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the unique sunny line passing through \\(P_i\\)\nand through no other point of \\(S_n\\).  \n(The slope can be chosen arbitrarily, e.g. \\(\\ell_i\\) can be taken as the line\nthrough \\(P_i\\) and \\((i+1,\\,n+1-i)\\); this slope is \\(-1\\) only when\n\\(i=n\\), which never occurs because \\(k\\le n-2\\).)  \nThus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_0 = S_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered; \\(|U_0|=\\binom{n+1}{2}-k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe now use \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to cover \\(U_0\\).\n\n*Step\u202fA (vertical lines).*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n-1\\) draw the vertical line\n\\(x=c\\).  \nEach such line contains the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); all\nthese points belong to \\(U_0\\) because the only points on the outer diagonal\nwith \\(x\\le k\\) have already been removed.  \nThe number of vertical lines used is \\((n-1)-(k+1)+1 = n-k-1\\).\n\n*Step\u202fB (the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).*  \nFinally draw the diagonal line \\(d:x+y=n+1\\).  \nIt contains the \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\); among them the\nfirst \\(k\\) have already been covered by the sunny lines, so \\(d\\) adds the\nremaining \\(n-k\\) points of the outer diagonal to the covered set.\n\nNow count the lines used:\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines,\n* \\(1\\) diagonal line.\n\nThe total is \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\) lines, as required.\n\nAll points of \\(U_0\\) are covered:\n\n* Points with \\(x\\ge k+1\\) lie on one of the vertical lines of Step\u202fA.\n* Points on the outer diagonal with \\(x>k\\) lie on the diagonal \\(d\\).\n* Points with \\(x\\le k\\) and not on the outer diagonal have already been\ncovered by the sunny lines.\n\nThus we have produced a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nwith precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nIf \\(k=n-1\\) then only one non\u2011sunny line is available.\nBy Section\u202f1 any non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\),\nwhereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence the covering condition cannot be satisfied.\n\nIf \\(k=n\\) there is no non\u2011sunny line at all, and a sunny line can pass\nthrough at most two points of \\(S_n\\); consequently at most \\(2n\\) points\ncould be covered, which is again insufficient because \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\)\nfor \\(n\\ge5\\) (and a direct check shows the failure for \\(n=3,4\\) as well).\n\nTherefore \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition (1) and the explicit construction for every\n\\(k\\) satisfying it together give a complete characterisation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
