ation uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   If the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** among the non‑sunny lines, each of the\n   \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, so at most\n   \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are covered by them.  \n   A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise the two\n   points would determine a slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Hence the total\n   number of covered points is at most \\((n-k)(n-1)+2k\\).  \n   Requiring this to be at least \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   If the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) **is** one of the non‑sunny lines, it contributes\n   \\(n\\) points, the remaining \\(n-k-1\\) non‑sunny lines at most \\(n-1\\) each,\n   and the same inequality again gives \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\) (points on the outer diagonal).\n   Through each \\(P_i\\) draw a distinct sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) that does **not**\n   pass through any other point of \\(S_n\\) (this is always possible because we\n   may choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the\n   slopes of the already drawn sunny lines).  \n   The \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct and each covers exactly the point\n   \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   - For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n-1\\) draw the vertical line\n     \\(x=c\\).  There are \\(n-k-1\\) such lines; each contains all points\n     \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), all of which are still uncovered.\n   - Finally draw the diagonal \\(d:\\;x+y=n+1\\).  This line contains the\n     \\(n\\) points of the outer diagonal; the first \\(k\\) of them have already\n     been covered by the sunny lines, so \\(d\\) adds the remaining \\(n-k\\) points.\n\n   The total number of lines used is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{diagonal})\n   =n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) is now covered:\n   * points with \\(x\\ge k+1\\) lie on one of the vertical lines,\n   * points on the outer diagonal with \\(x>k\\) lie on \\(d\\),\n   * points with \\(x\\le k\\) are exactly the \\(P_i\\) and are covered by the\n     sunny lines.\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) only one non‑sunny line is available; even the best possible\n   non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, while \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\)\n   for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Thus the covering condition cannot be met.  \n   If \\(k=n\\) there is no non‑sunny line.  A sunny line can contain at most two\n   points of \\(S_n\\); consequently at most \\(2n\\) points could be covered, which\n   is again insufficient because \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\) for all \\(n\\ge5\\) and a\n   direct check shows the failure for \\(n=3,4\\) as well.\n\nCombining the necessary bound and the explicit construction yields the full\ncharacterisation.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
