*\n\n3. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \n   Write the line as \\(b=ma+d\\) with rational \\(m,d\\) (the coefficients can be\n   taken rational because the line passes through integer points).  \n   For two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the differences \\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(b_2-b_1\\) cannot be\n   equal in absolute value; therefore the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\)\n   must have pairwise distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates and also distinct\n   \\(b\\)-coordinates.  \n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most one of the\n   numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) can appear as an\n   \\(a\\)-coordinate of a point of \\(\\ell\\); otherwise two of them would sum to\n   at most \\(n+1\\) and give the same \\(b\\)-coordinate, contradicting the\n   distinct‑\\(b\\) property.  Hence\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   Let \\(p\\) be the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the\n   non‑sunny lines.  Each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so\n   \\[\n   p\\le (n-k)n .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The remaining points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By (1) each\n   sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points,\n   whence the number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because all \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) have to be covered, (2) and\n   (3) give\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n+1}{2}\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   A short elementary calculation (separating the cases \\(n\\) even and odd)\n   shows that the inequality forces\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n   \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – the outer diagonal.*  \n   Draw the diagonal\n   \\[\n   d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   It belongs to the non‑sunny family \\(\\mathcal D\\) and contains the\n   \\(n\\) points\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny lines covering the first \\(k\\) points of \\(D\\).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) choose a slope \\(m_i\\) different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes already used, and draw the\n   line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x+(n+1-i)-m_i i .\n   \\]\n   By construction \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point\n   \\(P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\in D\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\) (the\n   chosen slope guarantees that the line does not meet any other lattice point\n   with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  The \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct and are sunny.\n\n   *Step 3 – vertical lines covering the remaining points.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n-1\\) draw the vertical line\n   \\[\n   v_c:\\;x=c .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(v_c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\) and contains exactly the points\n   \\[\n   \\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n   All these points are still uncovered because their \\(x\\)-coordinate is\n   \\(\\ge k+1\\) and they are not on the diagonal \\(d\\) (the diagonal points have\n   \\(x\\le n\\) but those with \\(x\\ge k+1\\) are already covered by the vertical\n   lines).  The number of such vertical lines is\n   \\[\n   (n-1)-(k+1)+1=n-k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4 – counting the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines used is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{diagonal})\n   =n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5 – verification of coverage.*  \n   - Points with \\(x\\ge k+1\\) belong to the vertical line \\(v_x\\).  \n   - Points on the diagonal with \\(x>k\\) belong to the diagonal \\(d\\).  \n   - The points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) belong to the sunny lines\n     \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines,\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Excluding the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) there is only one non‑sunny line.  Even the best possible\n   non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points, while \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\)\n   for all \\(n\\ge3\\); therefore the covering condition cannot be satisfied.  \n   If \\(k=n\\) there are no non‑sunny lines.  By (1) each sunny line contains at\n   most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, so at most\n   \\(n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points can be\n   covered, again impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary bound with the explicit construction yields the full\ncharacterisation.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
