            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the two requirements **iff**\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n   \\(\\mathcal V:\\;x=c\\) (vertical), \\(\\mathcal H:\\;y=c\\) (horizontal) and  \n   \\(\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c\\) (diagonal).\n\n2. **Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a non‑sunny line.**  \n   *Vertical* line \\(x=c\\) contains the points \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points, and the bound \\(n\\) is attained for \\(c=1\\).  \n   *Horizontal* lines are analogous.  \n   *Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) contains the points \\(\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1,\\; a\\le n+1-c\\}\\); the largest possible cardinality is again \\(n\\), attained for the outer diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every non‑sunny line contains at most }n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A sharp bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line that meets at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+d\\) with rational slope \\(m\\) and rational intercept \\(d\\) (the coefficients are rational because the line passes through integer points).  \n   Suppose \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the numerator and denominator cannot be equal in absolute value; otherwise \\(m=\\pm1\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a_2-a_1\\neq |b_2-b_1| .\\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3.1) we deduce that the set of \\(a\\)-coordinates of points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is **strictly increasing** and that the corresponding set of \\(b\\)-coordinates is also strictly increasing (otherwise two points would have the same \\(b\\) and (3.1) would be violated).  \n\n   Because each point satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the smallest possible sum of the first two distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates is \\(1+2=3\\); the largest possible sum of the last two distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates is \\((n-1)+(n)=2n-1\\). The monotonicity forces the sequence of \\(a\\)-coordinates to be a subset of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\):  \n\n   *Proof.* Assume, for contradiction, that there exist two distinct points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) whose \\(a\\)-coordinates are \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) with \\(p\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor<q\\). Then \\(b\\)-coordinates are \\(b_p=n+1-p\\) and \\(b_q=n+1-q\\) (the maximal possible values compatible with the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Since \\(q> \\frac{n+1}{2}\\), we have \\(b_q<\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le p\\). Consequently \\(b_q<b_p\\) while \\(p<q\\), contradicting the monotonicity of the \\(b\\)-coordinates. Hence all \\(a\\)-coordinates lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).  \n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   and the bound is attained, for example, by the line \\(y=x\\) (which is not sunny) when \\(n\\) is odd, or by the line \\(y=x+1\\) when \\(n\\) is even; in any case (3.2) is the best possible for sunny lines.\n\n4. **Necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Let \\(p\\) be the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the non‑sunny lines. By (2.1) each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\le (n-k)n .\\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points, \\(|S_n|-p\\), must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines. By (3.2) each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-p\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4.1) and (4.2) and using \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t\\):* then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\). The inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   t(2t+1)\\le (2t-k)2t+kt\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 2t^{2}+t\\le 4t^{2}-2kt+kt\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 0\\le 2t^{2}-(k-1)t .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(t\\ge 2\\) (because \\(n\\ge3\\)), the right‑hand side is non‑negative only when \\(k\\le 2t-2=n-2\\).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t+1\\):* then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\). The inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1-k)(2t+1)+k(t+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 2t^{2}+3t+1\\le 4t^{2}+2t+1-2kt+k(t+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 0\\le 2t^{2}-(k-2)t .\n   \\]\n\n   Again, because \\(t\\ge1\\), we must have \\(k\\le 2t-1=n-2\\).\n\n   Thus in both parity cases  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is strict for \\(k=n-1\\) and