le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All these points are still uncovered because their \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(k+1\\) and they are not among the diagonal points \\(P_i\\) with \\(i\\le k\\).  \n\n   The number of such vertical lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)-(k+1)+1=n-k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5.4 – counting the lines.*  \n   Total number of lines used:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }\\ell_i}\n   +\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{vertical }v_c}\n   +\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal }d}\n   =n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5.5 – verification of coverage.*  \n   - If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge k+1\\), then it lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\) (i.e. the point is on the outer diagonal), it lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) unless \\(a\\le k\\); for \\(a\\le k\\) the point is exactly \\(P_a\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n   - No other points of \\(S_n\\) exist. Hence every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Excluding the impossible cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) there is only one non‑sunny line. By (2.1) that line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, whereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\); thus the covering condition cannot be satisfied.  \n   If \\(k=n\\) there are no non‑sunny lines. By (3.2) each sunny line covers at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, so at most \\(n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\\) points can be covered, again impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary bound with the explicit construction yields the full characterisation.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, genera