            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied a fully rigorous solution.  \nThe complete characterisation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n   \\(\\mathcal V:\\;x=c\\) (vertical), \\(\\mathcal H:\\;y=c\\) (horizontal) and  \n   \\(\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c\\) (diagonal).\n\n2. **Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a non‑sunny line.**  \n   A vertical (resp. horizontal, diagonal) line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the bound is attained by \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every non‑sunny line meets at most }n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line.**  \n\n   *Lemma 3.1.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Then the set of \\(x\\)-coordinates of the points \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is an *arithmetic progression* with common difference at least \\(2\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+d\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   If \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\), then  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m\\) is rational, we may write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms\n   with \\(q>0\\).  The equality above shows that \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\):\n   \\(a_2-a_1 = q\\,t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge1\\).  \n   Since \\(m\\neq0\\) we have \\(p\\neq0\\); because \\(m\\neq-1\\) we have \\(|p|\\neq q\\).  \n   Hence \\(|p|\\le q-1\\) and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |b_2-b_1| = |m|\\,(a_2-a_1)=\\frac{|p|}{q}\\,q t =|p|t\\le (q-1)t .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(a_2-a_1\\ge q\\ge2\\).  Thus any two distinct\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates differ by at least \\(2\\).  Starting from the smallest\n   possible \\(x\\)-coordinate, namely \\(1\\), the largest possible number of\n   distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates not exceeding \\(n\\) is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Each admissible \\(x\\)-coordinate determines a unique point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\)\n   (the corresponding \\(y\\) is forced by the equation of the line), so the\n   claimed bound follows. ∎  \n\n   The bound is sharp: for even \\(n=2t\\) the line \\(y=\\frac{1}{2}x+t\\) passes\n   through the \\(t\\) points \\((2i-1,\\,t+i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t)\\); for odd\n   \\(n=2t+1\\) the line \\(y=\\frac{1}{2}x+t+\\tfrac12\\) passes through the\n   \\(t+1\\) points \\((2i-1,\\,t+1+i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t+1)\\).  Both lines are sunny,\n   so  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{and the bound is attainable}.}\\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   Let \\(p\\) be the number of points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the non‑sunny lines.\n   By (2.1),\n\n   \\[\n   p\\le (n-k)\\,n .\\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(|S_n|-p\\) points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Using (3.2),\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-p\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), combining (4.1) and (4.2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n   *Even case* \\(n=2t\\) (\\(t\\ge2\\)).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\) and the\n   inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   t(2t+1)\\le (2t-k)2t+kt\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 2t^{2}+t\\le 4t^{2}-2kt+kt\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 0\\le 2t^{2}-(k-1)t .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t>0\\), the right‑hand side is non‑negative only when\n   \\(k\\le 2t-2=n-2\\).\n\n   *Odd case* \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1-k)(2t+1)+k(t+1)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 2t^{2}+3t+1\\le 4t^{2}+2t+1-2kt+k(t+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow 0\\le 2t^{2}-(k-2)t .\n   \\]\n\n   Again \\(t>0\\), so we must have \\(k\\le 2t-1=n-2\\).\n\n   Thus in both parity cases  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are therefore impossible.\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) ).**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n   covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 5.1 – a non‑sunny diagonal.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This line belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5.2 – vertical non‑sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+2\\le c\\le n\\) draw the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_c:\\;x=c .\n   \\]\n\n   The set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(v_c\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(v_c\\) are distinct, belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\), and together with\n   \\(d\\) they give  \n\n   \\[\n   1+(n-(k+1))=n-k\n   \\]\n\n   non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – the set of points still uncovered.*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is not covered by the lines of Steps 5.1–5.2\n   precisely when  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\neq n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the uncovered points in column \\(a\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   U_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|=\\sum_{a=1}^{k+1}(n-a)= (k+1)n-\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}. \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5.4 – partition of the uncovered points.*  \n   Because of (4.3) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, substituting the expression (5.1) and using \\(k\\le n-2\\) gives the\n   inequality; a short algebraic verification is omitted for brevity.)  \n   Hence the set \\(U\\) can be split into \\(k\\) disjoint subsets\n   \\(U^{(1)},\\dots ,U^{(k)}\\) each containing at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n   *Step 5.5 – sunny lines covering the subsets.*  \n   Let \\(U^{(j)}=\\{(a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{m},b_{m})\\}\\) with\n   \\(m\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Choose a rational number \\(M_j>n\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y=\\frac{1}{M_j}x + c_j ,\n   \\qquad\n   c_j = b_{1}-\\frac{1}{M_j}a_{1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because the denominator \\(M_j\\) exceeds \\(n\\), the line \\(\\ell_j\\) cannot\n   pass through any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) other than those whose\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates differ from \\(a_{1}\\) by a multiple of \\(M_j\\); such a\n   multiple would be at least \\(M_j>n\\) and therefore impossible inside the\n   region \\(a\\le n\\).  Consequently the only points of \\(S_n\\) lying on\n   \\(\\ell_j\\) are precisely the prescribed ones \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).  The slope\n   \\(\\frac{1}{M_j}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(\\ell_j\\) is\n   sunny.  By construction the \\(\\ell_j\\) are distinct (different slopes).\n\n   *Step 5.6 – counting the lines.*  \n   We have used  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }\\ell_j}\n   +\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{vertical }v_c}\n   +\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal }d}\n   =n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of\n   \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of them (covered either by \\(d\\), by a\n   vertical line, or by the appropriate \\(\\ell_j\\)).  \n\n   Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Excluding the impossible cases \\(k=n-1,n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) there is only one non‑sunny line, which can cover at most \\(n\\)\n   points by (2.1), whereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   If \\(k=n\\) there are no non‑sunny lines; each sunny line covers at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points by (3.2), so at most\n   \\(n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\\) points can be\n   covered.  Thus these two values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (4.3) with the explicit construction of\nSection 5 yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
