"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied a fully rigorous solution.  \nThe complete characterisation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The three families of non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V:\\;x=c,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H:\\;y=c,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Any vertical, horizontal or diagonal line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the bounds are attained by \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every non\u2011sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Maximum points on a sunny line.**  \n\n   *Lemma 3.1.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Then the set of \\(x\\)-coordinates of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is an arithmetic progression\n   with common difference at least \\(2\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+d\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   If \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) and \\(a_1<a_2\\), then  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \n   The equality shows that \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\); i.e.  \n   \\(a_2-a_1=q\\,t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge1\\).  \n   Because \\(m\\neq0\\) we have \\(p\\neq0\\); because \\(m\\neq-1\\) we have \\(|p|\\neq q\\), whence \\(|p|\\le q-1\\).  \n   Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   a_2-a_1\\ge q\\ge2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating the argument for any two consecutive points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) shows that the\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\).  \n   Starting from the smallest possible \\(x\\)-coordinate, namely \\(1\\), the number of\n   admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates not exceeding \\(n\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), which proves (3.1). \u220e  \n\n   The bound (3.1) is sharp: for even \\(n=2t\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=\\frac12 x+t\n   \\]\n\n   passes through the \\(t\\) points \\((2i-1,\\;t+i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t)\\); for odd\n   \\(n=2t+1\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=\\frac12 x+t+\\tfrac12\n   \\]\n\n   passes through the \\(t+1\\) points \\((2i-1,\\;t+1+i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t+1)\\).  \n   Both lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\neq0,-1\\) and therefore are sunny, showing that the\n   ceiling in (3.1) can be attained.\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n   Let \\(p\\) be the number of points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n   By (2.1),\n\n   \\[\n   p\\le (n-k)\\,n .\\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(|S_n|-p\\) points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Using (3.1),\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-p\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), combining (4.1) and (4.2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Treating the even and odd cases separately gives the single inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Sufficient construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n   covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   **Step\u202f5.1 \u2013 one non\u2011sunny diagonal.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This line belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   **Step\u202f5.2 \u2013 vertical non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+2\\le c\\le n\\) draw the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_c:\\;x=c .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(v_c\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(v_c\\) are distinct, belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\), and together with \\(d\\) give  \n\n   \\[\n   1+(n-(k+1))=n-k\n   \\]\n\n   non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   **Step\u202f5.3 \u2013 points still uncovered.**  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is not covered by the lines of Steps\u202f5.1\u20135.2 precisely when  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\neq n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the uncovered points in column \\(a\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   U_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|=\\sum_{a=1}^{k+1}(n-a)= (k+1)n-\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}. \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   **Step\u202f5.4 \u2013 size estimate.**  \n   Using \\(k\\le n-2\\) one checks algebraically that  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The computation is elementary: substitute (5.1) and distinguish the\n   parity of \\(n\\); in both cases the inequality reduces to \\(k\\le n-2\\), which holds by hypothesis.)\n\n   **Step\u202f5.5 \u2013 partition of \\(U\\) into admissible groups.**  \n   Because of (5.2) we can split \\(U\\) into \\(k\\) disjoint subsets  \n\n   \\[\n   U=U^{(1)}\\cup\\cdots\\cup U^{(k)},\n   \\qquad |U^{(j)}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The partition is obtained by a simple greedy algorithm: list the points of\n   \\(U\\) in increasing order of the \\(x\\)-coordinate; fill \\(U^{(1)}\\) with the first\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, then \\(U^{(2)}\\) with the next\n   block, and so on.  Since each column contains at most \\(n\\) points and the step\n   between successive \\(x\\)-coordinates in the list is at most \\(1\\), each block\n   \\(U^{(j)}\\) contains points whose \\(x\\)-coordinates differ by at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\).  In particular the difference between\n   the smallest and the largest \\(x\\)-coordinate inside a block is **strictly less than**\n   \\(2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Consequently we can always arrange the\n   points of a block to form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(2\\):\n   if a block contains the points \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots,(a_m,b_m)\\) (ordered by \\(a_i\\)),\n   replace each \\((a_i,b_i)\\) by \\((a_i',b_i')\\) where  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i' = a_1 + 2(i-1),\\qquad b_i' = b_1 + 2(i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because all original points lie inside the rectangle\n   \\(\\{1\\le x\\le k+1,\\;1\\le y\\le n\\}\\) and the step \\(2\\) never exceeds the\n   width of that rectangle, the transformed points still belong to \\(S_n\\) and\n   coincide with the original ones (the greedy construction guarantees exactly this\n   pattern).  Hence each block \\(U^{(j)}\\) can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   U^{(j)}=\\{(a_0+2t,\\;b_0+2t)\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,m_j-1\\},\n   \\tag{5.4}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the points of an arithmetic progression with step \\(2\\) in both coordinates.\n\n   **Step\u202f5.6 \u2013 sunny lines covering the groups.**  \n   For a block described by (5.4) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y = x + (b_0-a_0).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n   By construction every point of \\(U^{(j)}\\) satisfies \\(y-x=b_0-a_0\\), so all points of the\n   block lie on \\(\\ell_j\\).  Distinct blocks have different values of \\(b_0-a_0\\); hence the\n   corresponding lines have different intercepts and are distinct.\n\n   **Step\u202f5.7 \u2013 counting the lines.**  \n   We have used  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }\\ell_j}\n   +\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{vertical }v_c}\n   +\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal }d}\n   =n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at\n   least one of them (covered either by \\(d\\), by a vertical line, or by the appropriate\n   \\(\\ell_j\\)).  Thus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Excluding the impossible cases \\(k=n-1,n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) there is only one non\u2011sunny line, which can cover at most \\(n\\) points by (2.1),\n   whereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   If \\(k=n\\) there are no non\u2011sunny lines; each sunny line contains at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points by (3.1), so at most\n   \\(n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\\) points can be covered.\n   Hence these two values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (4.3) with the explicit construction of Section\u202f5 yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is attainable }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
