            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=0,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\; x=0,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\; x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) (the case \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) is analogous).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nSince both points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1\\le (n+1-a_2)-(n+1-a_1)=a_1-a_2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\le -1 .\n\\]\n\nIf the inequality is strict, then \\(m<-1\\) and the line is parallel to neither \\(\\ell_1\\) nor \\(\\ell_2\\) nor \\(\\ell_3\\); however the equality case \\(m=-1\\) means the line is parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \nThus the only way for a sunny line to contain two points of \\(T_n\\) would be to have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemma 1  \n\nWith \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total, the number of points that can be covered by the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-k=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed ordinate \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nHence every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct and each is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Argument  \n\nSuppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k>0\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-k=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nBut each non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\); any such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (because the abscissa or ordinate of a point of \\(T_n\\) is at most \\(n\\)).  \nThus the total number of points that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n = n^2-nk .\n\\]\n\nWe would need  \n\n\\[\nn^2-nk \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - k ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\ge (n-1)k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the left‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\((n-1)k\\) for any \\(k\\ge 1\\), a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nBy Lemma 2 a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines does exist (the \\(n\\) horizontal lines).  \n\nHence the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
