 the maximum of \\(n+1-m\\) is attained at \\(m=1\\) and equals \\(n\\). However the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n\\), and the pair \\((1,n)\\) is allowed only when \\(n\\ge 2\\). More precisely, for any integer \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nn+1-m\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(m\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) then \\(n+1-m\\ge\\frac{n}{2}+1\\), but the points \\((m,b)\\) must also satisfy \\(b\\ge 1\\); the worst case occurs when \\(m\\) is as close as possible to \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\), giving at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n*Horizontal lines* are symmetric, giving the same bound.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) for some constant \\(d\\).  \nIf \\(d\\) is not a positive integer, again there are no points of \\(S_n\\) on it.  \nIf \\(d=m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n+1\\), the points of \\(S_n\\) on the line are exactly the solutions of  \n\n\\[\na+b=m,\\qquad a,b\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m-1\\) such solutions, but the additional restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(m\\le n+1\\). The maximum of \\(m-1\\) under the condition \\(m\\le n+1\\) is \\(n\\), and as before the same parity argument shows that at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) of these points can lie in \\(S_n\\). Formally,\n\n\\[\nm-1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) we obtain:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting the contribution of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\n\\]\n\nare non\u2011sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 each of those \\(t\\) lines can cover at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Using the covering requirement\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, i.e. the set \\(S_n\\) is a subset of the union of the \\(n\\) lines. Consequently the number of points that the non\u2011sunny lines collectively cover cannot be smaller than \\(|S_n|\\). From (1) and (4) we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\binom{n+1}{2}\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides of (5) by \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Since \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le n/2\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)/2}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\n      \\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{n}=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nA sharper estimate is obtained by treating the parity of \\(n\\) separately.\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot m\\ge m(2m+1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\ge 2m+1=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot m\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n      \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n      t\\ge 2m+2 = n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus in every case  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, so (6) can hold only if some points of \\(S_n\\) are covered by more than one line. The inequality (6) is nevertheless the *necessary* lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines required to achieve the covering.\n\nRe\u2011expressing (6) in terms of \\(k=n-t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n+1)= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible. The resolution is that the estimate (4) is not always tight: a non\u2011sunny line may cover fewer than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points, and different non\u2011sunny lines may share points, allowing the total number of distinct covered points to be smaller than the product in (4). Therefore we must relax (4) to a *necessary* inequality rather than an equality.\n\nA correct necessary condition follows from the observation that each non\u2011sunny line can cover **at most** \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) *new* points (points not already covered by previous non\u2011sunny lines). Hence after using \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines we can have covered at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(S_n\\). To cover all \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) points we must have  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. This gives a necessary lower bound on the admissible values of \\(k\\). The problem of determining whether every integer \\(k\\) satisfying this bound can actually be realized, and whether additional restrictions (upper bounds) exist, remains open."
