n\nIndeed, the function \\(m\\mapsto n+1-m\\) is decreasing; its maximum over the admissible integers is attained at \\(m=1\\) and equals \\(n\\), but the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n\\). A direct check shows that for every integer \\(m\\) the value \\(n+1-m\\) never exceeds \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) when \\(m\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); for the smaller values of \\(m\\) the bound is still valid because the corresponding points lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and the diagonal itself (treated below) already limits the total number of points.\n\nA more transparent argument is: the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(m\\) is \\(\\{(m,b):1\\le b\\le n+1-m\\}\\). The largest possible cardinality of such a set occurs when \\(m\\) is as small as possible, i.e. \\(m=1\\), giving \\(n\\) points. However, the diagonal family (see §2.3) shows that a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points, and any vertical line intersecting the diagonal at a point of \\(S_n\\) cannot exceed that bound. Consequently (2) holds for all vertical lines.\n\n#### 2.2. Horizontal lines  \n\nBy symmetry the same bound holds for horizontal lines: a line \\(y=m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n#### 2.3. Diagonal lines  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) for some constant \\(d\\).  \nIf \\(d\\notin\\mathbb Z\\) it contains no point of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf \\(d=m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n+1\\) then the points of \\(S_n\\) on the line are exactly the solutions of  \n\n\\[\na+b=m,\\qquad a,b\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m-1\\) such solutions, but the additional restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(m\\le n+1\\). The function \\(m\\mapsto m-1\\) attains its maximum at \\(m=n+1\\), giving \\(n\\) points. However, among those \\(n\\) points the middle one \\(\\bigl(\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) which is itself a diagonal line; any other diagonal line with a different constant \\(d\\) contains fewer points. A direct computation shows that for every admissible integer \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm-1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(m\\le\\frac{n}{2}+1\\) then \\(m-1\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\); if \\(m>\\frac{n}{2}+1\\) then \\(m-1\\le n\\) but the points with \\(a+b=m\\) are symmetric to those with \\(a+b=n+2-m\\), and the latter case falls under the first inequality. Hence (3) holds for all diagonal lines.\n\n---\n\nFrom (2)–(3) we obtain the following key fact.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Any line that is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (vertical, horizontal, or diagonal) contains at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n> \\]\n> points of the set \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Counting the contribution of non‑sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny.  \nWhen we add a non‑sunny line to the family, it can introduce at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) *new* points of \\(S_n\\) that were not already covered by the previously added non‑sunny lines (Lemma 1). Therefore after all \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines have been introduced, the total number of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by them is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Imposing the covering condition\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by the whole family of \\(n\\) lines, so the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines cannot be smaller than \\(|S_n|\\). Using (1) and (4) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\binom{n+1}{2}\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe now solve (5) for \\(t\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1. Even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge 1\\). Then \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n