le numbers of sunny\nlines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions\nof the problem, and that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nBecause the family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, therefore not sunny, and they are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nConsider the subset  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) then \\(T\\) is empty and no sunny line is needed.  \nAssume \\(k\\ge 1\\).  For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x-\\bigl(n-2k+2i-1\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nThe constants \\(c_i=n-2k+2i-1\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_i\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T\\)\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T\\) there exists a unique index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)\nsuch that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad b\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na = n-k+