            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions\nof the problem, and that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nBecause the family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, therefore not sunny, and they are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nConsider the subset  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) then \\(T\\) is empty and no sunny line is needed.  \nAssume \\(k\\ge 1\\).  For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x-\\bigl(n-2k+2i-1\\bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nThe constants \\(c_i=n-2k+2i-1\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_i\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T\\)\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T\\) there exists a unique index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)\nsuch that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (4).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom the definition of \\(T\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad b\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nRearranging the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-(k-1)\\le a-b\\le k-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=\\frac{a-b-n+2k+1}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (6) the numerator in (7) is an even integer lying between\n\\(2\\) and \\(2k\\); consequently \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\n\nEquation (7) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\na-b = n-2k+2i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely equation (4).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_i\\).\n\nIf a point satisfied (4) for two different indices \\(i\\neq j\\), then we would\nhave \\(a-b = n-2k+2i-1 = n-2k+2j-1\\), forcing \\(i=j\\), a contradiction.\nThus the index \\(i\\) is unique. ∎\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The whole family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the required conditions\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  The set contains \\(n-k+k=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  By construction the only sunny lines are the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(S_i\\); therefore exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of the lattice points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which belongs to\n  \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in T\\), and by the Lemma it lies on the\n  sunny line \\(S_i\\) for a suitable \\(i\\).\n\nThus every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one line\nof \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Non‑existence for other values of \\(k\\)\n\nIf \\(k>n\\) we would need more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, which is impossible\nbecause the family contains only \\(n\\) lines.  If \\(k<0\\) the statement is\nmeaningless.  Hence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\nexactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe construction above shows that each such \\(k\\) is attainable, and the\nobvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no other value is possible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
