ssary condition\n\nSince the family must contain exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence any feasible \\(k\\) must\nsatisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, therefore not sunny, and they are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) then \\(T=\\varnothing\\) and no sunny line is needed.  Assume\n\\(k\\ge 1\\).  For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x-\\bigl(n-2k+2j-1\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny (it is\nneither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  The constants\n\\(c_j=n-2k+2j-1\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma (coverage of the triangle \\(T\\))\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor every point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) there exists a unique index \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)\nsuch that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom \\((a,b)\\in T\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad b\\ge 1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\na-b\\le n-1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na-b\\ge (n-k+1)-\\bigl(n+1-(n-k+1)\\bigr)=n-2k+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le a-b\\le n-1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe interval in (6) contains exactly the \\(k\\) odd integers  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1,\\;n-2k+3,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently there is a unique integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na