 k\\le n\\)) take the \\(k\\) points\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n   which lie on the top diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a slope \\(m_i\\) satisfying  \n\n   * \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (so the line will be sunny);\n   * the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct;\n   * the line \\(L_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) with slope \\(m_i\\) does **not** pass through any other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the set of slopes that would make the line pass through another lattice point of \\(S_n\\) is finite (it is the solution set of finitely many linear equations). Hence we can choose the \\(m_i\\) inductively, avoiding a finite forbidden set at each step. Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct, sunny, and each contains exactly the point \\(P_i\\) from \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Covering the remaining points with non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   S_n' = S_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every point of \\(S_n'\\) still satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and is not on the top diagonal.  \n   We now cover \\(S_n'\\) using only non‑sunny lines, employing exactly \\(n-k\\) of them:\n\n   *Take the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines*  \n   \\[\n   V_j : x=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   *Take the first \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines*  \n   \\[\n   D_t : x+y = t\\qquad (t=2,\\dots ,n-k+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The collection  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{\\text{non‑sunny}}=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{\\,n-k+1}\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(2(n-k)\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   From these we delete any \\(n-2k\\) lines (possible because \\(k\\le n\\)) that are redundant for covering \\(S_n'\\); a convenient choice is to keep all the vertical lines and discard the diagonal lines with the largest constants. The remaining set contains exactly \\(n-k\\) lines and still covers every point of \\(S_n'\\):  \n\n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n'\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   * If \\(a> n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\). Hence \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is among the kept diagonal lines (their constants run from \\(2\\) up to \\(n-k+1\\)).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n'\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal L_{\\text{non‑sunny}} .\n   \\]\n   The set \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; the first \\(k\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny. By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n6. **Exhaustiveness.**  \n   Because we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
