F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{M\\}\n> \\]\n> still covers \\(T_n\\) and all its lines remain distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers \\(T_n\\), the line \\(L\\) contains at least one point of \\(T_n\\); choose one and denote it by  \n\n\\[\nP=(a,b)\\in L\\cap T_n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal F|=n\\ge 3\\) and only one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is being removed, there exists another line \\(L'\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L\\}\\).  \nThe set \\(L'\\cap T_n\\) is non‑empty (otherwise \\(L'\\) would be useless for the covering, contradicting the minimality of the family; we may simply discard such a line, but the hypothesis guarantees at least one useful line).  \nPick a point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(a',b')\\in L'\\cap T_n,\\qquad Q\\neq P .\n\\]\n\nConsider the line \\(M\\) determined by the two distinct points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) lie on two different lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\), the slope of \\(M\\) cannot be equal to the slope of either of those two lines.  \nIf the slope of \\(M\\) were \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), then \\(M\\) would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, and consequently the two original lines \\(L\\) and \\(L'\\) would also be parallel to that direction (they both contain one of the points and share the same direction). That would force \\(L\\) and \\(L'\\) to be parallel, contradicting the fact that the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are distinct (two distinct parallel lines cannot both contain the same point of \\(T_n\\) because the points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates and the distance between parallel lines with slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) is an integer multiple of \\(\\sqrt2\\); a more elementary argument is that if two distinct lines of slope \\(0\\) (or \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)) both contain a lattice point, they must be the same line).  \nHence the slope of \\(M\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. \\(M\\) is sunny.\n\nNow we verify the covering property.  \nEvery 