            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\binom{n+1}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line can be of one of the three types in (5).\n\n*Horizontal.*  A line \\(y=b_{0}\\) with \\(b_{0}\\ge1\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,b_{0})\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{0}\\); at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Vertical.*  A line \\(x=a_{0}\\) with \\(a_{0}\\ge1\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) in the points \\((a_{0},b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_{0}\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  A line \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge2\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); thus at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.\n\nHence **any** non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines in a family that covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \nFrom (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer and \\(n\\ge3\\), the smallest possible value of \\(t\\) is \\(n-1\\).  \nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-(n-1)=n-2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1 A base configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2-k),\\\\[2pt]\nD_j &: x+y=n+1-j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,\\,k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical or slope \\(-1\\) lines, hence non‑sunny.  \nTheir total number is \\((n-2-k)+k=n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: y=2x+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny.\n\nWe claim that the \\(n\\) lines (7)–(8) cover every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2-k\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) contains the point.\n\n*If \\(a> n-2-k\\)*, write \\(a=n-1-k+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+2-r\\).\n\n* Subcase \\(b=k+2-r\\).  \n  Then \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{r-1}\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le r-1\\le k\\)).\n\n* Subcase \\(b<k+2-r\\).  \n  In this situation \\(b\\le k+1-r\\).  \n  The point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the strict inequality  \n\n  \\[\n  b<\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n  \\]\n\n  because the right–hand side equals \\(\\tfrac12 (n-1-k+r)+\\tfrac{n+2}{2}=k+2-\\tfrac{r}{2}\\) and \\(b\\le k+1-r<k+2-\\tfrac{r}{2}\\).  \n  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies strictly below \\(\\ell_{1}\\).  \n  It also satisfies \\(b<2a+1\\), so it lies below \\(\\ell_{2}\\) as well.  \n  The only points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are \\((n-1,1)\\) (on \\(\\ell_{1}\\)) and \\((1,3)\\) (on \\(\\ell_{2}\\)); both belong to the previous subcases with equality, so they are already covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (7)–(8).  \nThe configuration therefore uses exactly two sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2 Reducing the number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the configuration above, we may replace a sunny line by a non‑sunny line without losing the covering property:\n\n* Replace \\(\\ell_{1}\\) by the vertical line \\(V_{n-1-k}\\) (which is already present when \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n  The point \\((n-1,1)\\) that was previously covered only by \\(\\ell_{1}\\) now lies on this vertical line, while all other points remain covered.\n\n* Replace \\(\\ell_{2}\\) by the diagonal line \\(D_{k+1}\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=n-k\\)).  \n  The point \\((1,3)\\) is then covered by this diagonal line, and again no other point loses a covering line.\n\nEach replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nPerforming the replacement \\(2-k\\) times (when \\(k\\le2\\)) or, more generally, replacing any subset of the two sunny lines by additional non‑sunny lines of the forms (7) yields a configuration with **exactly** the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\nSince we can start from the base configuration (which has two sunny lines) and decrease the sunny count step‑by‑step down to \\(0\\), we obtain, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (6) sh