\\]\n   and because \\(b\\ge 1\\) we actually get \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n'\\) belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-k}\\) defined in (2).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-k}\\) contains exactly \\(2(n-k)\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   We now delete any \\(n-2k\\) of them (possible because \\(k\\le n-2\\) implies \\(n-2k\\ge 0\\)) and keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines.  \n   Deleting lines does not destroy the covering property: each point of \\(S_n'\\) is covered either by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) or by a diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\); both types are present among the retained lines because we only discard lines with the *largest* constants, i.e. vertical lines with \\(x>n-k\\) (which do not exist) and diagonal lines with constant \\(>n-k+1\\) (which are not needed).  \n\n   Thus we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n'\\).\n\n   *Step 4.3 \u2013 final family.*  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=n\\)**  \n\n   Take the \\(n\\) points \\((i,1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a distinct sunny slope \\(m_i\\) (again avoiding the finitely many forbidden slopes that would make the line pass through another point of \\(S_n\\)).  \n   Let \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\((i,1)\\) with slope \\(m_i\\).  \n   As before each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the point \\((i,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The set \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) therefore consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\) because every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\); in particular the point \