f1 each sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(kn\\) points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  Any such line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points as well (the longest possible intersection is the side of the right\u2011angled triangle \\(S_n\\)).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+(n-k)n=n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 1\\), the inequality imposes **no restriction** beyond the obvious one \\(k\\le n\\).  Consequently the only necessary condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.1 \u2013 the sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S_n .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_i\\) lie on the top diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(m_i\\) satisfying  \n\n   * \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (so the line will be sunny);\n   * the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct;\n   * the line \\(L_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) with slope \\(m_i\\) does **not** pass through any other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the set of slopes that would make the line meet another point of \\(S_n\\) is finite (it consists of the slopes determined by \\(P_i\\) and each of the finitely many other points of \\(S_n\\)).  Hence we can pick the \\(m_i\\) inductively, always avoiding a finite forbidden set.  \n   The resulting lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct, sunny, and each contains exactly the point \\(P_i\\) from \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.2 \u2013 the non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Remove the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(