            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n\n*Case 1: \\(m\\) irrational.*  \nThen the line meets the integer lattice \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) in at most one point, so the bound holds.\n\n*Case 2: \\(m\\) rational.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFor a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) on the line we have  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{p}{q}a+c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad qa= p a+qc .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(q\\mid a\\); write \\(a=qt\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\).  \nSubstituting gives  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+ c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) must be an integer, the intercept \\(c\\) must be of the form \\(c=r/q\\) with \\(r\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+\\frac{r}{q}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\mid r .\n\\]\n\nThus we may write \\(c=s/q\\) with \\(s\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+s .\n\\]\n\nConsequently every lattice point of the line is of the shape  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(qt,\\;pt+s),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nqt+pt+s\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le\\frac{n+1-s}{p+q}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p+q\\ge1\\) and \\(s\\ge0\\) (otherwise \\(b\\le0\\) contradicts positivity), the right‑hand side is at most \\(\\frac{n}{p+q}\\le n\\).  Hence the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) – and therefore the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on the line – does not exceed \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the requirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then obviously \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  No further restriction follows from Lemma 1, because each line (sunny or not) can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThus the only necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 1. Sunny lines\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nx_i=n-k+i,\\qquad P_i=(x_i,\\;n+1-x_i)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(P_i\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates and lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\) we choose a slope \\(m_i\\) such that  \n\n* \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\);\n* the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct;\n* the line \\(L_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) with slope \\(m_i\\) contains no other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the set of slopes that would make the line pass through another point of \\(S_n\\) is finite (it consists of the slopes determined by \\(P_i\\) and each of the finitely many other points of \\(S_n\\)).  Hence we can select the \\(m_i\\) inductively, always avoiding a finite forbidden set.  Consequently the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct, sunny, and each contains exactly the point \\(P_i\\) from \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2. Non‑sunny lines\n\nRemove the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(S_n\\) and denote  \n\n\\[\nS'_n=S_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that every point \\((a,b)\\in S'_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\nIndeed, suppose \\(a>n-k\\).  Then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  Using the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le (n-k+1)+k=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand equality can hold only when \\((a,b)=(n+1-i,i)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\), a point that has been removed.  Therefore no point of \\(S'_n\\) can have \\(a>n-k\\).\n\nConsequently each remaining point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).  The family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}:=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines (all non‑sunny) and together they cover every point of \\(S'_n\\).\n\n#### 3. The whole family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the points \\(P_i\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(L_i\\), all other points by the vertical lines).  Thus a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCondition (1) is necessary, and the construction above shows it is also sufficient.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log