}\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and,\nby the injectivity just observed, contains at most one element for each\n\\(a\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n\\cap\\ell|\\le n .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the problem’s\nrequirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then obviously  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nNo further restriction follows from Lemma 1: each line (sunny or not) can\ncover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\), while  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2\\qquad (n\\ge1),\n\\]\n\nso the total number of points can always be accommodated by \\(n\\) lines.\nThus (3.1) is the **only** necessary condition.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1. Choice of \\(k\\) special points  \n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nx_i=n-k+i,\\qquad\nP_i=(x_i,\\;n+1-x_i)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(P_i\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and have distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates \\(x_i\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.2. Sunny lines through the special points  \n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) consider all slopes determined by \\(P_i\\) and any other\npoint of \\(S_n\\setminus\\{P_i\\}\\); there are finitely many such slopes.\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_i=\\bigl\\{\\,\\text{slope of the line }P_iQ\\mid Q\\in S_n,\\;Q\\neq P_i\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery slope in \\(\\mathcal F_i\\) would make a line through \\(P_i\\) pass through\nanother lattice point of \\(S_n\\).  Since \\(\\mathcal F_i\\) is finite, we can\nchoose a real number \\(m_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_i .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(L_i\\) to be the line through \\(P_i\\) with slope \\(m_i\\).\nBecause of (4.1),\n\n* \\(L_i\\) is sunny;\n* \\(L_i\\) contains **no** point of \\(S_n\\) other than \\(P_i\\).\n\nWe select the slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) inductively, each time avoiding the\nfinite set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_i\\) and also avoiding the slopes\nalready used, so the resulting lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 4.3. Remaining points and vertical lines  \n\nRemove the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(S_n\\) and denote  \n\n\\[\nS'_n=S_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Claim.** Every \\((a,b)\\in S'_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(a>n-k\\).  Then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  Using\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a+b\\le (n-k+1)+k=n+1\\), and equality holds only when\n\\((a,b)=(n+1-i,i)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\), which has been removed.\nThus no point of \\(S'_n\\) can have \\(a>n-k\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each point of \\(S'_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}:=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct\nvertical lines (all non‑sunny) and together they cover **all** points of\n\\(S'_n\\).\n\n#### 4.4. The whole family of lines  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.* \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.* Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n- Each special point \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\) and to no other line.  \n- Every other point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to some vertical line in\n  \\(\\mathcal V_{\\,n-k}\\) by the claim proved above.\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have constructed a configuration satisfying both\nrequirements of the problem.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nCondition (3.1) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) is necessary, and the construction above\nshows it is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of\nsunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
