umber of points of \\(S_n\\) not belonging to \\(C\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-|C|\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\n= k\\bigl(n-\\tfrac{k-1}{2}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nIn particular this number is **at most** \\(k\\) (the inequality follows because \\(n\\ge k\\)). Hence we can select a set  \n\n\\[\nU\\subseteq S_n\\setminus C,\\qquad |U|=k,\n\\]\n\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) uncovered points.\n\n**Step 3 – sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\((a,b)\\in U\\) choose a sunny line \\(S_{a,b}\\) passing through \\((a,b)\\) as in Section 3 (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). Because the points of \\(U\\) are distinct and the slopes are chosen distinct, the lines \\(S_{a,b}\\) are distinct and none coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n**Step 4 – the final family.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\mathcal N\\cup\\{S_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in U\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines. By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) (if it belongs to \\(C\\)) or on a line of the sunny set (if it belongs to \\(U\\)). Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{a,b}\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non‑sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies all the required conditions with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Sections 2, 3, 4).\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (inequality (2)).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
