            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and the set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point\n\\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines’’ is exactly that the family of lines\ncovers the finite set \\(T_n\\).  A simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  All other lines are\n**non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) there are\nat least two non‑sunny lines; equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nCall a line **horizontal**, **vertical** or **diagonal** according as its slope\nbelongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Suppose, for a contradiction, that at most one\nof the three kinds occurs in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Case 1 – no horizontal line.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(P\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates; a non‑horizontal line can\ncontain at most one of them.  Hence covering the \\(n\\) points of \\(P\\) would\nrequire at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑horizontal lines, i.e. all lines of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) must be used and each of them meets exactly one point of \\(P\\).\n\nNow look at  \n\n\\[\nQ:=\\{(n+1-b,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(Q\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates; a non‑vertical line can\ncontain at most one of them.  Because we assumed there is also no vertical\nline, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can meet at most one point of \\(Q\\) as well.\nBut the two sets \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) intersect in the single point\n\\((\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2})\\) when \\(n\\) is odd and are disjoint when \\(n\\) is\neven.  Consequently at least one line would have to contain two distinct\npoints of \\(P\\cup Q\\), contradicting the observation above.  Hence the\nassumption “no horizontal line’’ is impossible.\n\n*Case 2 – no vertical line* and *Case 3 – no diagonal line* are completely\nsymmetric (swap the roles of the axes or rotate the picture by \\(45^{\\circ}\\)).\nThus each of the three possibilities leads to a contradiction, and at least\ntwo of the three forbidden directions must appear among the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines altogether, the number of sunny lines is at\nmost \\(n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound of Lemma 1 is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction)  \nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\)\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe build \\(\\mathcal L\\) in three stages.\n\n---\n\n**Stage A – two non‑sunny lines that cover many points.**  \n\nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H:\\; y=1\n\\]\n\nand the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_V:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to the forbidden set, hence are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with either coordinate equal to \\(1\\) lies on one of\nthese two lines.  The set of uncovered points after Stage A is  \n\n\\[\nU_1:=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA simple description of \\(U_1\\) is  \n\n\\[\nU_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|U_1|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Stage B – \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of \\(U_1\\).**  \n\nList the points of \\(U_1\\) arbitrarily as\n\\((p_1,q_1),\\dots,(p_{|U_1|},q_{|U_1|})\\).\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct points among them; denote them\n\\((p_{i_1},q_{i_1}),\\dots,(p_{i_k},q_{i_k})\\).\n\nFor each chosen point we draw a line through it whose slope is a rational\nnumber different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes already used.\nBecause there are infinitely many admissible slopes, we can select the slopes\nso that all \\(k\\) lines are distinct and sunny.  Denote these lines by\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\).\n\nEach \\(\\ell_j\\) covers its designated point and possibly other points of\n\\(U_1\\); this will be harmless for the final step.\n\n---\n\n**Stage C – filling the remaining gap with non‑sunny diagonal lines.**  \n\nAfter adding the \\(k\\) sunny lines, some points of \\(U_1\\) may still be\nuncovered.  Let  \n\n\\[\nU_2:=U_1\\setminus\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\ell_j .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U_2\\) is empty we are done (the total number of lines is\n\\(2+k\\le n\\); we will add dummy lines later).  Otherwise \\(U_2\\) is a finite\nset of lattice points, each with coordinates at least \\(2\\).\n\nObserve that any two distinct points of \\(U_2\\) have different\n\\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates (otherwise they would lie on\nthe same vertical or horizontal line, which are already present).  Hence the\nline joining any two points of \\(U_2\\) has slope different from \\(0\\) and\n\\(\\infty\\).  Moreover, because all points of \\(U_2\\) satisfy\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\), the slope of any such line cannot be \\(-1\\):\nif the slope were \\(-1\\) we would have \\(a+b\\) constant, contradicting the\nstrict inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with the lower bound \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).\n\nConsequently **every** line determined by two points of \\(U_2\\) is a\nnon‑sunny line (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is excluded).  We now cover \\(U_2\\) by\nusing at most \\(|U_2|-1\\) such lines: pick an arbitrary point of \\(U_2\\) as a\nbase, and for each of the remaining points draw the line through the base\nand that point.  All these lines are parallel to each other (they have the\nsame slope) and belong to the forbidden family “diagonal”, hence are\nnon‑sunny.  Denote the collection of these lines by\n\\(\\mathcal D\\); its size is at most \\(|U_2|-1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Counting the lines.**  \n\nSo far we have\n\n* 2 lines from Stage A,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines from Stage B,\n* at most \\(|U_2|-1\\) diagonal lines from Stage C.\n\nBecause the points of \\(U_2\\) are a subset of \\(U_1\\), we have\n\\(|U_2|\\le |U_1|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n2+k+ (|U_2|-1)\\le 2+k+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2-1\n                =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 +k+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k\\le n-2\\) this quantity is **strictly less than** \\(n\\) when\n\\(n\\ge 5\\); for the small cases \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection shows that the\nsame inequality holds (the right‑hand side equals \\(n\\) or less).  Therefore\nthe total number of lines constructed so far does not exceed \\(n\\).\n\nIf the number is **strictly smaller** than \\(n\\) we add the required number of\nextra diagonal lines (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) that do not pass through any\npoint of \\(T_n\\); this is always possible because a diagonal line can be\ntranslated arbitrarily.  These added lines are non‑sunny and do not affect the\ncoverage of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering all points of \\(T_n\\),\nand containing precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists\na configuration attaining that value.  Hence the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
