ement holds.\n\n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible.  \n   No other values are possible because we are required to have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; thus \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nsatisfy (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). For any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) we have\n\\(a=i\\) and \\(a+b=i+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((i,b)\\in T_n\\) and lies on \\(L_i\\).\nConversely, if \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the\nvertical line \\(L_a\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and satisfying (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any set \\(S\\) of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; for concreteness we may take  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{\\,y=m_j x+b_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere each slope \\(m_j\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (so the line is sunny) and the pairs \\((m_j,b_j)\\) are distinct.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\bigr\\}\\cup S .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\) are vertical, hence non‑sunny; the lines in \\(S\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the vertical lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\}\\) already cover every point of \\(T_n\\); adding the sunny lines does not affect this covering.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies (1) and has the required numbers of sunny and non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nNo value \\(k>n\\) or \\(k<0\\) can occur.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Since a line is either sunny or not sunny, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\), and obviously \\(k\\ge 0\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma 2 we obtain that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable.\nLemma 3 shows that no other integer can occur. Therefore the set of all admissible\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
