ay take  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{\\,y=m_j x+b_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere each slope \\(m_j\\) lies in \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (so the line is sunny) and the pairs \\((m_j,b_j)\\) are distinct.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\bigr\\}\\cup S .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\) are vertical, hence non‑sunny; the lines in \\(S\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the vertical lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\}\\) already cover every point of \\(T_n\\); adding the sunny lines does not affect this covering.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies (1) and has the required numbers of sunny and non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nNo value \\(k>n\\) or \\(k<0\\) can occur.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Since each line is either sunny or not sunny, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\), and obviously \\(k\\ge 0\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable, and Lemma 3 shows that no other integer can occur. Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
