            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(y=\\text{const}\\) (horizontal), \\(x=\\text{const}\\) (vertical) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) (diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nThe family of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{L_i : x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(a\\) is a positive integer and\n\\(a\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\nequation \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(L_a\\in\\mathcal V_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, which is one of the three\nforbidden directions. Consequently none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) is\nsunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is admissible; i.e. there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the family \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) from Lemma 1.  \nIt consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\) by Lemma 1,\nand contains no sunny line by Lemma 2. Hence it meets all requirements with\n\\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the case \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nTo obtain a configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) one must replace at least one of the\nvertical lines in \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) by a line that is **sunny**.  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical, so it cannot contain all points \\((a,b)\\) with\na fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\) when there are at least two such points\n(i.e. for every \\(a\\le n-1\\)). Consequently a simple replacement of a vertical\nline by a sunny line does **not** preserve the covering property.\n\nA possible approach would be to design a set of sunny lines that together\ncover the points that lose their vertical line, while the remaining vertical\nlines continue to cover the rest of \\(T_n\\). This requires a careful\npartition of \\(T_n\\) into subsets each lying on a single sunny line, and a\nverification that the total number of lines remains exactly \\(n\\). At present\nI have not succeeded in constructing such a partition for any \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe problem is completely solved for \\(k=0\\); the existence of configurations\nwith \\(k>0\\) remains an open question. The admissible set of \\(k\\) therefore\ncontains at least \\(\\{0\\}\\). Further work is needed to determine whether all\nintegers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as spec