            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (intersection of a non‑vertical line with a column)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** vertical. Then for every \\(a\\) the set\n\\(\\ell\\cap C_a\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nFix \\(a\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives a unique value \\(y=ma+q\\). Hence at most\none point of the form \\((a,\\,\\cdot\\,)\\) can lie on \\(\\ell\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (columns that need a vertical line)\n\nIf \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\) then any family of lines covering all points of \\(C_a\\) must\ncontain a vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the contrary: all lines covering \\(C_a\\) are non‑vertical.  \nBy Lemma 1 each such line contributes at most one point of \\(C_a\\); therefore\nthe total number of points of \\(C_a\\) that can be covered is at most the number\nof lines used. Since the lines are required to be distinct, covering at least\ntwo points would need at least two distinct non‑vertical lines, each intersecting\n\\(x=a\\) at a different point. But two distinct non‑vertical lines cannot share\nthe same \\(x\\)-coordinate, because two distinct lines of the form \\(y=m_i x+q_i\\)\n intersect at a single point; they cannot both pass through two different points\nwith the same \\(x\\). Hence it is impossible to cover \\(|C_a|\\ge2\\) without a\nvertical line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (necessary number of vertical lines)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) must contain the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|C_a| = n+1-a \\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2 each such column forces the presence of the vertical line \\(x=a\\)\nin the covering family. Hence the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\)\nare unavoidable. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 1 (bound on sunny lines)\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) forced lines are vertical, they are not sunny.  \nThe remaining (the \\(n\\)-th) line may be vertical or non‑vertical.  \nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\nAll lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = 2x-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope is \\(2\\neq0\\) and \\(\\neq -1\\); it is not vertical. Hence it is not\nparallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it is sunny.\n\n*Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.*\n\n* For a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) we have, for any integer \\(b\\) with\n  \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\),\n\n  \\[\n  b\\neq 2a-2n+1\\quad\\text{because }2a-2n+1\\le -1<1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\((a,b)\\) does not lie on \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) and is covered by the\n  vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* The only point not belonging to any of those vertical lines is \\((n,1)\\);\n  substituting \\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) gives\n  \\(y=2n-2n+1=1\\). Thus \\((n,1)\\in L_{\\text{sun}}\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are therefore covered, and exactly one line (namely\n\\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\)) is sunny. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Corollary 1 we have \\(k\\le1\\).  \nThe explicit constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) can be\nrealised for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
