al line can contribute at most one point of \\(C_a\\);\nhence to cover all \\(|C_a|\\) points of that column we need at least \\(|C_a|\\)\ndifferent (non‑vertical) lines.  Summing over the \\(n-v\\) remaining columns we\nobtain the lower bound  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge v+\\sum_{a=v+1}^{n}|C_a|\n   =v+\\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-v}t\n   =v+\\frac{(n-v)(n-v+1)}{2}.\n\\] ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 1 (minimum number of vertical lines)  \nIf exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\), then at least \\(n-1\\) of them are\nvertical.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) in Lemma 2 and denote \\(m=n-v\\).  The inequality becomes  \n\n\\[\nn\\ge v+\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}=n-m+\\frac{m(m+1)}{2},\n\\]\ni.e. \\(-m+\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\le 0\\), which simplifies to \\(m(m-1)\\le 0\\).\nThus \\(0\\le m\\le 1\\), whence \\(v=n-m\\ge n-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for sunny lines  \n\nVertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.\nCorollary 1 shows that among the \\(n\\) required lines at most one can be\nnon‑vertical, and only a non‑vertical line may be sunny.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence constructions  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(L_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\).  \nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and thus lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nNo line is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=2x-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\)\nis sunny.  \n\nFor any column \\(a\\le n-1\\) and any admissible \\(b\\) we have\n\\(b\\ge 1>2a-2n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\notin L_{\\text{sun}}\\) and is covered by the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe sole point not belonging to a vertical line is \\((n,1)\\); substituting\n\\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) yields \\(y=1\\), so this point\nlies on \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly\none line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.\nSince sunny lines are non‑vertical, we would have at least two non‑vertical\nlines.  Together with the \\(n-2\\) remaining lines we would have at most\n\\(n-2\\) vertical lines, contradicting Corollary 1, which requires at least\n\\(n-1\\) vertical lines.  Hence \\(k\\ge 2\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion  \n\nThe only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
