\\) (the largest possible\n   ordinate in \\(T_n\\) is \\(n\\)).  However, many of the horizontal lines may\n   coincide with the already used vertical or sunny lines only if their slope\n   were \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), which never happens because the horizontal lines\n   have slope \\(0\\) while the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0\\).  Thus\n   the total number of distinct lines is exactly \\(v+m=n\\).\n\nHence the required family exists. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (adjusting the number of sunny lines)  \nWith the notation of Lemma 3, for any integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le m\\)\nthere exists a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn the construction of Lemma 3 the \\(m\\) non‑vertical lines are all sunny.\nReplace any \\(m-k\\) of them by horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) (or by lines of slope\n\\(-1\\)); the point \\((v+j,1)\\) that each replaced line originally covered is\nstill covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  The remaining \\(k\\) lines keep\ntheir sunny slopes, so exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.  All other lines (the\nvertical ones and the horizontal lines introduced for higher ordinates) are\nunchanged, therefore the covering property persists. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of all possible numbers of sunny lines  \n\nFrom Lemma 2 we know that if exactly \\(m\\) lines are non‑vertical then at least\n\\(n-m\\) lines are vertical; consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed\n\\(m\\).  Lemma 3 shows that for every \\(m\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a covering with\nexactly \\(m\\) non‑vertical lines exists, and Lemma 4 shows that within such a\ncovering we can realise any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\).\n\nTherefore the set of attainable values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{m=0}^{n}\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth extremal values are realised:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\);\n* \\(k=n\\): take \\(m=n\\) (no vertical lines) and keep all \\(n\\) non‑vertical lines\n  sunny as in Lemma 3.\n\nHence every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive can occur, and no other\nvalue is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
