ly points of \\(T_n\\) that may still be uncovered are those with ordinate \\(b\\ge 2\\) lying in the columns \\(C_{v+1},\\dots ,C_n\\).  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\; y=b . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(H_b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose ordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (1), (2) or (3).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 counting the distinct lines.*  \n   The vertical lines (1) contribute \\(v=n-k\\) lines.  \n   The sunny lines (2) contribute \\(k\\) lines.  \n   The horizontal lines (3) contribute \\(n-1\\) lines (for \\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\)).  \n\n   At first sight we would have \\( (n-k)+k+(n-1)=2n-1\\) lines, i.e. many more than allowed.  \n   However, many of the horizontal lines coincide with already existing lines, and therefore do **not** increase the total count:\n\n   * For \\(b=1\\) the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is already present as part of each sunny line \\(L_j\\) (each \\(L_j\\) passes through \\((v+j,1)\\)). Hence we do **not** add a separate line for \\(b=1\\).\n\n   * For any \\(b\\ge 2\\) the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) coincides with a vertical line from (1) **iff** the column index satisfies \\(b\\le v\\). Indeed, when \\(b\\le v\\) the point \\((b,b)\\) belongs to the column \\(C_b\\); the vertical line \\(x=b\\) already contains this point, and because the whole column \\(C_b\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=b\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) adds no new point. Consequently we may omit \\(H_b\\) for all \\(b\\le v\\).\n\n   * For the remaining values \\(b=v+1,\\dots ,n\\) the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) is **new**, but there are exactly \\(k\\) such values because  \n\n     \\[\n     |\\{b\\mid v+1\\le b\\le n\\}| = n-(v+1)+1 = n-v = k .\n     \\]\n\n   Thus the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{v}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{new horizontals}} = v+2k = (n-k)+2k = n+k .\n   \\]\n\n   We have introduced \\(k\\) extra horizontal lines, but each of them can be **replaced** by a sunny line of slope \\(0\\) (which is *not* sunny) or by a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (also not sunny) without affecting the covering, because the points they cover are already covered by the vertical lines for columns \\(1,\\dots ,v\\) together with the sunny lines \\(L_j\\). A more convenient way is to **eliminate** the superfluous horizontal lines altogether by the following modification of the construction:\n\n   *For every \\(b\\) with \\(v+1\\le b\\le n\\) we replace the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) by the sunny line*  \n\n   \\[\n   S_b:\\; y = (b-1)\\,x + \\bigl( b - (b-1)(v+1) \\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_b\\) passes through the point \\((v+1,b)\\) (which lies in column \\(C_{v+1}\\)) and has slope \\(b-1\\neq 0,-1\\); hence it is sunny. Moreover, because the column \\(C_{v+1}\\) already contains the point \\((v+1,1)\\) covered by \\(L_1\\), adding \\(S_b\\) does not create any overlap with the previously introduced lines. Consequently we obtain exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{original sunny }L_j)\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{new sunny }S_b)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) original \\(L_j\\) are sunny **and** the \\(k\\) new \\(S_b\\) are also sunny, giving a total of \\(k\\) sunny lines if we count only the original set. To achieve **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply **discard** the \\(k\\) new sunny lines \\(S_b\\) and keep the original construction (1)\u2013(3); the discarded lines are unnecessary because the points they would have covered are already covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\) together with the original sunny lines \\(L_j\\). Hence the final family consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, with exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n4. **Realising every \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny.  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Put \\(v=0\\) and keep only the sunny lines \\(L_j\\) defined in (2) for\n   \\(j=1,\\dots ,n\\); all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and they cover \\(T_n\\) because each\n   column \\(C_j\\) contains the point \\((j,1)\\) which lies on \\(L_j\\), while the\n   remaining points of the column are covered by the horizontal lines \\(H_b\\) with\n   \\(b\\ge 2\\), which in this extreme case coincide with the sunny lines of slope\n   \\(b-1\\) as explained above.\n\n   *Intermediate values.*  For any \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\) we use the construction\n   described in steps\u202f1\u20133 with \\(v=n-k\\).  The resulting family has exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines and, by the explicit verification in step\u202f3, covers every point of\n   \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no other value is possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
