operty. A key lemma shows that a sunny line can be introduced in place of a non‑sunny line without losing any covered point, provided the sunny line passes through a point that was previously covered only by the line being replaced. By iterating this replacement we obtain configurations with exactly any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nFinally we prove that no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, because each sunny line can cover at most one point of the set \\(\\{(a,b):a+b=n+1\\}\\) and there are exactly \\(n\\) such points. Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non‑sunny line is parallel to one of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary\n\nEach line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIndeed, a line parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\) (horizontal) has equation \\(y=c\\) with\n\\(1\\le c\\le n\\); it meets the triangle in the points \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points.  The same bound holds for\nvertical lines (\\(x=c\\)) and for lines parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) (equation\n\\(x+y=c\\)): they intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nConsequently, if we have \\(m\\) distinct lines, the total number of points\nthey can cover is at most \\(mn\\).  Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) by (1),\nwe must have  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad m\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are required to be distinct, a sharper bound is obtained\nby observing that the \\(n\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on\ndifferent lines parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); no two of them can be covered by\nthe same line unless that line is exactly \\(\\ell_d\\) shifted, which would\nmake it parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) and thus non‑sunny.  Hence at least \\(n\\)\ndifferent lines are needed.  Therefore any admissible configuration uses\nexactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  d_c:\\;x+y=c,\n  \\]\n\n  which is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  There are \\(n\\) such lines.\n\n* No horizontal or vertical lines are needed.\n\nWe claim that the family \\(\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Indeed, a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\); hence \\(c:=a+b\\) is an integer with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), and\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(d_c\\).  The lines are distinct because they\nhave different constants \\(c\\).  All of them are parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\),\nso none of them is sunny.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Introducing sunny lines\n\nWe now show how to replace a non‑sunny line by a sunny one without losing\ncoverage.  The following lemma is the key.\n\n**Lemma 3.1.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  Suppose\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny and there exists a point\n\\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies **only** on \\(\\ell\\) (i.e. no other line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) passes through \\(P\\)).  Then for any sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that\npasses through \\(P\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  All points of \\(T_n\\) different from \\(P\\) were already covered\nby lines other than \\(\\ell\\); they remain covered after the replacement.\nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by the new sunny line \\(\\ell'\\). ∎\n\nHence, to increase the number of sunny lines we only need to locate a\npoint of \\(T_n\\) that is uniquely covered by a non‑sunny line.  In the\nconfiguration of Section 2 such points are abundant: on the line\n\\(d_c\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) is the only point of \\(T_n\\) with\n\\(x=1\\) on that line, and no other line of the family passes through it.\nTherefore each line \\(d_c\\) contains a uniquely‑covered point.\n\nStarting from the \\(k=0\\) configuration, we perform the following\niterative procedure.\n\n*Step.*  Choose a remaining non‑sunny line \\(d_c\\).  Let\n\\(P_c=(1,c-1)\\) be its uniquely‑covered point.  Replace \\(d_c\\) by a sunny\nline \\(\\ell_c\\) that passes through \\(P_c\\) (for instance the line with\nslope \\(2\\) through \\(P_c\\)).  By Lemma 3.1 the covering property is\npreserved and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nRepeating the step for any subset of the \\(n\\) lines yields a configuration\nwith exactly as many sunny lines as the number of performed replacements.\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we obtain a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe “hypotenuse’’ of the triangle; it contains exactly \\(n\\) points:\n\\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); consequently two distinct\npoints of \\(S_n\\) cannot lie on the same sunny line (otherwise the line\nthrough them would be parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\)).  Hence each sunny line can\ncover **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).  Since all points of \\(S_n\\) must be\ncovered, the number of sunny lines is bounded above by \\(|S_n|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* At least \\(n\\) lines are required, and exactly \\(n\\) lines suffice.\n* There exists a covering with \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n* For every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a covering with exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n* No covering can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
