from \\(P\\) were already covered by\nlines other than \\(\\ell\\); they remain covered after the replacement.\nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by the new sunny line \\(\\ell'\\).  \u220e\n\nIn the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Section\u202f1 each line \\(d_c\\) contains\nthe point  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(1,c-1)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nNo other line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) passes through \\(P_c\\) because all other\nlines have a different constant term in the equation \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\nThus \\(P_c\\) is uniquely covered by \\(d_c\\), and Lemma\u202f2.1 applies.\n\nNow we construct, for any prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), a\ncovering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Construction.*  \nChoose any subset \\(C\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|C|=k\\).\nFor each \\(c\\in C\\) replace the line \\(d_c\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell_c\\) that\npasses through the unique point \\(P_c=(1,c-1)\\).  For instance we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\; y-(c-1)=2\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(2\\) (hence is not parallel to \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\))\nand clearly passes through \\(P_c\\).  Because the points \\(P_c\\) are distinct,\nthe newly introduced sunny lines are distinct from each other, and they are\nalso distinct from the remaining non\u2011sunny lines \\(d_{c'}\\) (\\(c'\\notin C\\))\nbecause the latter are parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) whereas the \\(\\ell_c\\) are not.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2.1 each replacement preserves the covering of \\(T_n\\).  After\nreplacing exactly the \\(k\\) lines indexed by \\(C\\) we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_c\\mid c\\in C\\,\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,d_{c'}\\mid\nc'\\notin C\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and\ncontains precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_c\\).  Hence every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No covering can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines\n\nConsider the set of points on the \u201chypotenuse\u2019\u2019 of the triangle:\n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n    =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) points in \\(H_n\\).\n\nIf a line is sunny, it is **not** parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  Any two distinct\npoints of \\(H_n\\) lie on a line parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of\n\\(H_n\\).  Since every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny\nlines in any admissible configuration cannot exceed \\(|H_n|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* a covering with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines;\n* for each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines;\n* an upper bound showing that no covering can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny\n  lines.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
