nd cannot all be covered by that single remaining line. Contradiction.\n\nThus at least two lines of the family belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\). ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the required number of non‑sunny lines}),\\qquad 2\\le m\\le n .\n\\]\n\nWe shall produce \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nChoose three distinct constants  \n\n\\[\nc_{1}=1,\\qquad c_{2}=n,\\qquad c_{3}=2 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: x=c_{1}\\quad\\text{(vertical line)},\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=c_{2}\\quad\\text{(horizontal line)},\\\\\nL_{3}&: x+y=c_{3}\\quad\\text{(line of slope }-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m=2\\) we keep only \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\); if \\(m\\ge 3\\) we keep all three.\nWhen \\(m>3\\) we add further vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,m-2\\) (all distinct\nand different from \\(c_{1}\\)). In total we obtain exactly \\(m\\) distinct\nnon‑sunny lines; all of them belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y = \\alpha_{t}x+\\beta_{t},\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(\\alpha_{t}\\) are chosen pairwise distinct and different\nfrom \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha_{t}=t\\)). The intercepts \\(\\beta_{t}\\) are\nchosen so that the line passes through the lattice point \\((t,\\,n+1-t)\\),\nwhich belongs to \\(S_{n}\\) because \\(t+(n+1-t)=n+1\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny and contains at least one point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_{1}\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=n\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{2}\\).  \n\n*If \\(a+b=2\\)*, i.e. \\((1,1)\\), it lies on the line \\(L_{3}\\) because\n\\(1+1=2=c_{3}\\).\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nWrite \\(a+b = s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\le k+1\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line\n\\(S_{s-1}\\) because \\(S_{s-1}\\) was defined to pass through \\((s-1,\nn+2-s)\\) and has slope \\(\\alpha_{s-1}\\neq 0\\); solving the linear equation\nshows that \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies it (the construction of the \\(\\beta_{t}\\)\nguarantees this).  \n\nIf \\(s>k+1\\) then \\(a\\le n-k-1\\). In this case the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\) is among the extra vertical lines added when \\(m>3\\); hence \\((a,b)\\)\nlies on that vertical line.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\)) and\n\\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nIf \\(k=n\\) then all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. By Lemma 1 at least two non‑sunny\nlines are necessary, so \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nIf \\(k=n-1\\) then exactly one line is non‑sunny. By Lemma 1 at least two\nnon‑sunny lines are required, hence \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of §2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\nis attainable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}}.\n\\]"
