oints.  \n   A greedy algorithm (always pick the line that covers the largest number of still\u2011uncovered points) finishes after at most \\(s\\) selections because each selection reduces the number of uncovered points by at least \\(n\\) and initially there are at most \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \n\n   Since \\(s=n-k\\) and \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\), a straightforward counting shows that the greedy algorithm never needs more than \\(s\\) lines; consequently we obtain exactly \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification.**  \n   By construction the total number of lines is \\(k+s=n\\), they are all distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (hence are sunny), and every lattice point of the triangle lies on at least one of the lines.  \n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1), and fails for any smaller \\(k\\) because of the counting argument in step\u202f3.  \n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those described in the boxed answer above.  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{2}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{3}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\,i\\mid L_{i}\\text{ is sunny}\\,\\},\\qquad s=n-k .\n\\]\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\): a horizontal line \\(y=r\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((1,r),\\dots ,(n+1-r,r)\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points; the same holds for vertical and diagonal lines.  \nConsequently the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(sn\\) points.\n\nA sunny line can intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points as well (a line of arbitrary slope can meet each of the \\(n\\) rows at most once). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(kn\\) points.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}| \\le sn+kn = n(s+k)=n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil \\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (3)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil \\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(s=n-k\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines that together cover \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The sunny lines  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}=i+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nso that each \\(m_{i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently any line with slope \\(m_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) pick a lattice point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in T_{n}\\) that has not been used before (this is possible because \\(|T_{n}|\\ge k\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x + (b_{i}-m_{i}a_{i}) .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) and, because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nBy construction each \\(S_{i}\\) contains at least one point of \\(T_{n}\\); it may contain more, but that is harmless.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU = T_{n}\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} S_{i}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered after the sunny lines have been placed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Covering the uncovered points with non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nConsider the three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* rows: \\(R_{r}: y=r\\) for \\(r=1,\\dots ,n\\);\n* columns: \\(C_{c}: x=c\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\);\n* diagonals: \\(D_{d}: x+y=d\\) for \\(d=2,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family.  \nHence each point of \\(U\\) lies on one row, one column and one diagonal.\n\nWe now select a sub\u2011collection \\(\\mathcal F\\) of these rows, columns and diagonals that covers all points of \\(U\\) and contains at most \\(s\\) lines.  \nThe selection proceeds greedily:\n\n```\nwhile U \u2260 \u2205 do\n      choose among all rows, columns, diagonals the one that contains the largest number of points of U;\n      add this line to \ud835\udd3d;\n      delete from U all points lying on the chosen line.\nend while\n```\n\nEach iteration removes at least one point, and in fact removes at least \\(n\\) points unless \\(|U|<n\\).  \nBecause initially  \n\n\\[\n|U|\\le |T_{n}| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nthe greedy algorithm finishes after at most  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{|U|}{n}\\right\\rceil \\le\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil = n-k = s\n\\]\n\niterations, where the last equality uses (3).  \nConsequently \\(|\\mathcal F|\\le s\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal F|<s\\) we may arbitrarily add further non\u2011sunny lines (for instance unused rows) until we have exactly \\(s\\) of them; this does not affect the covering property.\n\nDenote the resulting non\u2011sunny lines by \\(N_{1},\\dots ,N_{s}\\).  \nBy construction they are distinct (different rows, columns or diagonals are never equal) and each is parallel to one of the forbidden directions, i.e. non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Verification  \n\nThe total family  \n\n\\[\n\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}, N_{1},\\dots ,N_{s}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(k+s=n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(s\\) are not.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies either on some sunny line (by definition of \\(U\\)) or on a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\); hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (3) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines that meets the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the necessary condition (3) with the explicit construction for all admissible \\(k\\), we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers between \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is solved.**"
