ine the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x+b_{i},\\qquad \n   m_{i}=2+i\\;( \\neq0,\\infty,-1),\\qquad \n   b_{i}=r_{i}-m_{i}c_{i}.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((c_{i},r_{i})\\in T_{n}\\).  \n   The slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are all different and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently the \\(S_{i}\\) are distinct sunny lines.  \n   Moreover each \\(S_{i}\\) contains at least the point \\((c_{i},r_{i})\\).\n\n   **Uncovered points.**  \n   After placing the \\(k\\) sunny lines, the only points of \\(T_{n}\\) that may remain uncovered are those lying in rows that were **not** selected, i.e. the \\(s\\) rows  \n   \\[\n   y=r_{k+1},\\dots ,y=r_{n},\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\{r_{1},\\dots ,r_{n}\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take exactly those \\(s\\) unselected rows as the non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   N_{j}: y=r_{k+j}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,s).\n   \\]\n   They are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny, and together with the sunny lines they form a family of \\(k+s=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   *Coverage.* Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on some row \\(y=r\\).  \n   If \\(r\\) is one of the selected rows, the point is covered by the corresponding sunny line (the sunny line meets the whole row, possibly at several points).  \n   If \\(r\\) is an unselected row, the point is covered by the horizontal line \\(N_{j}\\) belonging to that row.  \n   Hence all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.\n\n   *Count of sunny lines.* By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(s\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Condition (1) is necessary, and the construction shows it is sufficient.  \n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2