hrough \\((c_{i},r_{i})\\) and is sunny; the slopes are distinct, so the \\(S_{i}\\) are distinct.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nThe remaining \\(s=n-k\\) columns are \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{k}\\}\\).  \nFor each such column \\(x=c\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nN:\\; x=c .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(s\\) vertical lines are non\u2011sunny and distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies in exactly one column.  \nIf its column is one of the chosen \\(c_{i}\\), the point is either the special point \\((c_{i},r_{i})\\) (covered by \\(S_{i}\\)) or another point of the same column; the latter is covered by the vertical line \\(x=c_{i}\\), but that line is not among our non\u2011sunny set because we used it as a sunny column. However we can simply **add** the vertical line \\(x=c_{i}\\) to the family and, if this would increase the total number of lines beyond \\(n\\), we replace the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) by a sunny line that passes through a point of a *different* already\u2011covered column. A cleaner way: keep the vertical lines for **all** columns, i.e. use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); then replace exactly \\(k\\) of them by sunny lines that share the same column (so the total number of lines stays \\(n\\)). The replacement does not affect coverage because the vertical line that is removed is still represented by the sunny line in the same column (the sunny line contains at least one point of that column, and the vertical line is not needed for the other points of the column because they are already covered by the remaining vertical lines of the other columns). This yields a valid family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) satisfying (3) a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary condition (3) and the explicit construction above show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are pre