, as argued below).\n\n5. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   H_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n        =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n   Any two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) lie on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   A sunny line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \n   Since every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(|H_n|=n\\).\n\n6. **The case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The previous construction yields at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines because the diagonal line \\(d\\) was kept non\u2011sunny.  \n   To obtain \\(k=n\\) we replace the diagonal line \\(d\\) itself by a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through any point of \\(H_n\\), say \\((1,n)\\).  \n   The point \\((1,n)\\) is still covered because it lies on the new sunny line \\(\\ell\\); all other points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (each of which was placed through a distinct point of \\(H_n\\)).  \n   The Lemma still applies because after removing \\(d\\) every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered by at least one of the vertical lines, and the added sunny line restores coverage of \\((1,n)\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining the construction for all \\(k\\) with the upper bound, we conclude that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A redundant covering with \\(n\\) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,d:\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) consists of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the diagonal line \\(d\\).  \nAll these lines are distinct; the vertical lines are parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and \\(d\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) (also non\u2011sunny).\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(d\\) and also on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (since \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\)).  \n\n* If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then \\(b\\le n-a\\le n-1\\). The point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). Although the horizontal line is not a member of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is also on the diagonal line \\(d\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the diagonal line contains all points with sum exactly \\(n+1\\); however we only need two lines from \\(\\mathcal C_n\\). Observe that the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is in \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) and the point \\((a,1)\\) (which has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate) lies on \\(d\\); consequently the vertical line together with \\(d\\) already cover \\((a,b)\\) via the fact that any vertical line intersects \\(d\\) at the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\), and the segment between \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the same vertical line. Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to both \\(x=a\\) and \\(d\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and also to the diagonal line \\(d\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) is a covering of \\(T_n\\) in which each point is covered at least twice.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma allowing replacement by a sunny line  \n\n**Lemma 2.1**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is a point that remains covered after removing \\(\\ell\\) (i.e. there exists \\(\\ell'\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) with \\(P\\in\\ell'\\)), then for any sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* After deleting \\(\\ell\\) every point of \\(T_n\\) is still on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) by the double\u2011coverage hypothesis. Adding the sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) restores coverage of \\(P\\) (which lies on \\(\\sigma\\)) and does not affect the coverage of any other point. Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction of configurations with any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nStart from the redundant covering \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(P_i\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and also on the diagonal line \\(d\\); therefore after removing the vertical line \\(x=i\\) the point \\(P_i\\) is still covered (by \\(d\\)).  \n\nChoose any set \\(I\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\) if \\(k\\le n-1\\); if \\(k=n\\) take \\(I=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and later also replace \\(d\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) select a sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that passes through \\(P_i\\) and whose slope is distinct from the slopes chosen for the other indices (this is always possible because there are infinitely many slopes).  \nReplace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2.1 the covering property is preserved after each replacement, because \\(P_i\\) remains covered by \\(d\\).\n\nAfter performing the replacements for all \\(i\\in I\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consisting of  \n\n* the diagonal line \\(d\\) (still present unless \\(k=n\\)),  \n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)), and  \n* the remaining vertical lines \\(x=j\\) for \\(j\\notin I\\).\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and, by construction, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n**The case \\(k=n\\).**  \nWhen \\(k=n\\) we have replaced all \\(n-1\\) vertical lines as above and still have the diagonal line \\(d\\).  \nReplace \\(d\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma_d\\) that passes through any point of \\(H_n\\) (for instance \\((1,n)\\)).  \nThe point \\((1,n)\\) is still covered because it lies on \\(\\sigma_d\\); all other points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (each \\(\\sigma_i\\) was chosen through a distinct point of \\(H_n\\)).  \nAgain Lemma\u202f2.1 guarantees that the covering is preserved.  \nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n     =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|H_n|=n\\).  \nAny two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) lie on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_n\\).  \nSince every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and the argument of Section\u202f4 shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

