h exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the newly inserted ones) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal). The total number of lines is \\(n\\) because we started from \\(n+1\\) lines and removed exactly one line, namely the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), which we now discard.\n\n4. **Obtaining \\(k=n\\).**  \n   After the previous step we have a family of \\(n\\) lines containing the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   The diagonal contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   H_n=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Each of the \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines constructed in step 3 passes through a distinct point of \\(H_n\\) (namely through \\((i,1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)).  \n   Replace the diagonal line by a sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Now every point of \\(H_n\\) lies on a sunny line, and all other points of the triangle are still covered because they were already covered by the vertical or horizontal lines that have not been removed. Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Upper bound.**  \n   The set \\(H_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) points, any two of which lie on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   A sunny line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \n   Since every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(|H_n|=n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps 3 and 4 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and step 5 shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible. Hence the admissible values are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A double covering with \\(n+1\\) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}}_{\\text{diagonal}} .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct; the vertical lines are parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\), the horizontal line is parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\), and the diagonal is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) it also lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); if \\(a=n\\) it lies on the diagonal because \\(a+b=n+1\\). Hence at least two lines contain the point.\n\n*Case 2: \\(b>1\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nMoreover \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies that the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal **or** can be moved vertically to a point on the diagonal while staying on the same vertical line.  \nIndeed the vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets the diagonal at \\((a,n+1-a)\\); because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the segment between \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is contained in the same vertical line. Consequently the diagonal line also contains the point \\((a,b)\\) when \\(b=n+1-a\\); if \\(b<n+1-a\\) the point is still covered by the vertical line and the diagonal line covers the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\), which is a different point of \\(T_n\\).  \nTo guarantee *double* coverage we observe that the point \\((a,1)\\) (which belongs to the same vertical line) is already on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) give two distinct lines passing through points of the same vertical line, and the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the vertical line while the horizontal line ensures that the vertical line is not the *only* line covering any point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA more direct argument is as follows: the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on both \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=1\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) (with \\(b>1\\)) lies on \\(x=a\\); thus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is never the *sole* line covering a point of \\(T_n\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is incident to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma for replacing a line\n\n**Lemma 2.1**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is a point that also belongs to another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThen for any sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Removing \\(\\ell\\) leaves every point of \\(T_n\\) covered at least once, because each point was on at least two lines and we removed only one of them. Adding \\(\\sigma\\) restores coverage of \\(P\\) (which lies on \\(\\sigma\\)) and cannot destroy coverage of any other point. Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Configurations with \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFrom Claim 1 we know that \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma 2.1.  \nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\).  \nFor each such line the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\in T_n\n\\]\n\nlies also on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); therefore \\(P_i\\) fulfills the condition of Lemma 2.1.\n\nChoose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nFor every \\(i\\in I\\) pick a sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that passes through \\(P_i\\) and whose slope is distinct from the slopes chosen for the other indices (this is always possible because there are infinitely many slopes).  \nReplace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \nBy Lemma 2.1 the covering property is preserved after each replacement, and the lines remain distinct because the slopes are distinct and none of the new lines coincides with the horizontal or diagonal line.\n\nAfter performing the replacements for all \\(i\\in I\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consisting of  \n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny),\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny),\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)),\n* the \\(n-1-k\\) untouched vertical lines \\(x=j\\) (\\(j\\notin I\\)).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nIn the construction of the previous section we may take \\(I=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); then all vertical lines have been replaced by sunny lines, and the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) consists of  \n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\),\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\),\n* the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)).\n\nEach sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\), which belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed \\((i,1)\\) is the endpoint of the segment of the diagonal line that joins \\((i,1)\\) to \\((i,n+1-i)\\); consequently the diagonal line contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains the other endpoint \\((i,1)\\).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cover the \\(n-1\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) of \\(H_n\\).\n\nNow replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma_d\\) that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\in H_n\\).  \nThe point \\((n,1)\\) is still covered (by \\(\\sigma_d\\)), and all other points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by the already existing sunny lines.  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because they were already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or by one of the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n\nConsequently we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider again the set  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \nAny two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) lie on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nA sunny line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \nSince every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(|H_n|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Final answer\n\nThe construction of Sections 3 and 4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and the argument of Section 5 shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
