            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nFor distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) the line through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((j,n+1-j)\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence it is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  \nA sunny line is **not** parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \nSince every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(|H_n|=n\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nk\\le n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A convenient covering with \\(n\\) lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{n-i}{i}\\,(x-i)+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) satisfy (5), so \\(L_i\\) is precisely the line through those points.  \nIts slope is \\(\\frac{n-i}{i}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\; y=1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1},L_n\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.2 (coverage of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\)).**  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(L_n\\).  \nIf \\(b>1\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nFor this \\(a\\) the line \\(L_a\\) passes through the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,n-a)\\); its equation is (5).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=a+1\\) gives \\(y=n-a\\).  \nBecause the points of the column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) lie on the straight segment joining \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,n-a)\\), every integer point of that column, in particular \\((a,b)\\), satisfies (5). Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\). ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) is a valid configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma for replacing a line\n\n> **Lemma 2.3.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of lines such that each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n> If \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\(P\\in\\ell\\) also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), then for any sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\) the family \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Removing \\(\\ell\\) leaves every point covered at least once, because each point had at least two incidences. Adding \\(\\sigma\\) restores coverage of \\(P\\) and does not affect any other point. ∎\n\nIn the family \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) every point \\((a,1)\\) lies on both \\(L_n\\) and \\(L_a\\); every point with \\(b>1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_a\\). Consequently each point of \\(T_n\\) is incident to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\), so the hypothesis of Lemma 2.3 is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(|I|=k\\).  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) do the following replacement:\n\n* let \\(P_i=(i,1)\\);\n* delete the line \\(L_i\\);\n* insert an arbitrary sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that passes through \\(P_i\\) (for instance, the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\(P_i\\)).\n\nBy Lemma 2.3 the covering property is preserved after each replacement, because \\(P_i\\) is also on the horizontal line \\(L_n\\).  \n\nAfter performing the replacements for all \\(i\\in I\\) we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,L_j\\mid j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Obtaining \\(k=n\\)\n\nTake \\(I=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) in the previous step; then all lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are replaced by sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_{n-1}\\).  \nThe current family consists of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) together with the horizontal line \\(L_n\\).\n\nNow replace the horizontal line \\(L_n\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma_n\\) that passes through the point \\((n,1)\\) (for example, the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\((n,1)\\)).  \nAgain Lemma 2.3 applies, because \\((n,1)\\) is already covered by \\(\\sigma_{n-1}\\) (the line \\(\\sigma_{n-1}\\) contains the whole column \\(x=n-1\\) and therefore also the point \\((n,1)\\) as its intersection with the horizontal