1 = 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((n-1,2)\\in L_n\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) we are in Case\u202f1.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma for replacing a line\n\n> **Lemma 2.3.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of lines such that each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n> If \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\(P\\in\\ell\\) also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), then for any sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\) the family \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemoving \\(\\ell\\) leaves every point covered at least once, because each point had at least two incidences. Adding \\(\\sigma\\) restores coverage of \\(P\\) and cannot destroy coverage of any other point. \u220e\n\n\n\nIn the family \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) every point \\((a,1)\\) lies on both \\(L_{n-1}\\) and \\(L_a\\); every point with \\(b>1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_a\\) (the one whose index equals its \\(x\\)-coordinate). Consequently each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to **at least two** lines of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\), so Lemma\u202f2.3 may be applied to \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) replace the sunny line \\(L_i\\) by an arbitrary sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that still passes through the point \\((i,1)\\) (for instance the line of slope \\(1\\) through that point).  \nBecause \\((i,1)\\) also lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{n-1}\\), Lemma\u202f2.3 guarantees that the covering of \\(T_n\\) is preserved after each replacement.\n\nAfter all replacements we obtain the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in I