            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nFor distinct \\(i\\neq j\\) the line through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((j,n+1-j)\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence it is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\), therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_n\\).  \nSince every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le |H_n| = n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x + 1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*   \\(S_1\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n*   For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(i-1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x = j . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical lines, therefore non‑sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\}\\cup\\{V_2,V_3,\\dots ,V_n\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal F_n\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.1 (coverage)\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case 1:* \\(b=1\\). Then \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Case 2:* \\(b\\ge 2\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-1\\).\n\n*   If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is \\(x=a\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*   If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\). Substituting \\(x=1\\) into the equation of \\(S_{b}\\) gives  \n\n    \\[\n    y = (b-1)\\cdot 1 + 1 = b ,\n    \\]\n\n    so \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{b}\\).\n\nThus every admissible lattice point is covered. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma for replacing a line\n\n> **Lemma 3.2.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of lines covering \\(T_n\\) such that each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n> If \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\(P\\in\\ell\\) also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), then for any line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\) the family \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\sigma\\}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemoving \\(\\ell\\) leaves every point still incident to at least one line (because each point had at least two incidences). Adding \\(\\sigma\\) restores the incidence of the distinguished point \\(P\\) and cannot remove incidences of any other point. Hence the covering property is preserved. ∎\n\n\n\nIn the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) each point of \\(T_n\\) is incident to at least two lines:\n\n* points with \\(b=1\\) lie on both \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_{a+1}\\) (the latter exists because \\(a\\le n-1\\));\n* points with \\(b\\ge 2\\) lie on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) and on the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\).\n\nTherefore Lemma 3.2 can be applied to \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) in this range.  \nChoose a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) replace the sunny line \\(S_i\\) by a new sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that still passes through the point \\((i,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; y = i\\,x + 1-i .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,1)\\) also belongs to the vertical line \\(V_i\\); consequently Lemma 3.2 guarantees that after each replacement the family continues to cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nAfter all replacements we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\sigma_i\\mid i\\in I\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{S_j\\mid j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{S_1\\}\\cup\\{V_2,\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\sigma_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the unchanged vertical lines together with the horizontal line \\(S_1\\)).  \nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) and perform the replacements described in step 4 for **all** indices \\(i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nAfter these replacements the only non‑sunny line left is the horizontal line \\(S_1\\).\n\nNow replace \\(S_1\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma_1\\) that passes through \\((1,1)\\); for example  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_1:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) also lies on the sunny line \\(S_2\\), so Lemma 3.2 applies and the covering is preserved.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines of the resulting family are sunny, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n-------------------------------------------