+y=c\\).\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) we have distinct \\(a\\)’s and distinct \\(b\\)’s as \\((a,b)\\) varies, so a horizontal line can meet \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, and the same holds for a vertical line.  \n\nA diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) either coincides with \\(x+y=s\\) (in which case it contains **all** points of \\(D_s\\)) or is disjoint from \\(D_s\\).  \n\nHence, unless the line \\(x+y=s\\) itself is included, each non‑sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\). Because \\(|D_s|=s-1\\ge 2\\) for \\(s\\ge 3\\) (and for \\(s=2\\) the single point can be covered by any line), covering all points of \\(D_s\\) without the diagonal would require at least \\(s-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines. Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total and there are \\(n\\) different values of \\(s\\), this is impossible unless the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) is present. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Proof\n\nAssume that for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.\n\nRemove the \\(k\\) sunny lines. By Lemma 1 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines meets a diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, unless it is the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) itself.  \n\nConsider a fixed diagonal \\(D_s\\). The \\(k\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_s\\). Therefore the other \\((s-1)-k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines, each of which can give at most one point of \\(D_s\\) unless it is the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\). Consequently the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) must belong to the family of non‑sunny lines.\n\nSince this argument holds for every \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), the set of non‑sunny lines must contain the \\(n\\) distinct diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThese already account for all \\(n\\) 