he line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\) are distinct, each is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{T}_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in L_c\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nSince a line is either sunny or not sunny, the number of sunny lines can never exceed the total number of lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nFix an integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(1,c-1)\\in L_c ,\\qquad Q_{c-1}=(2,c-3)\\in L_{c-2}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_c:=\\text{the line through }P_c\\text{ and }Q_{c-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{c-3-(c-1)}{2-1}= -2 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(S_c\\) is **sunny** (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \n\nObserve the following:\n\n* \\(P_c\\) belongs to the original diagonal \\(L_c\\); after the replacement \\(P_c\\) still lies on the new line \\(S_c\\).\n* \\(Q_{c-1}\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(L_{c-2}\\); that diagonal is still present in the family (we have not removed it yet).\n\nTherefore, after removing the diagonal \\(L_c\\) and inserting the sunny line \\(S_c\\) the covering property of the whole family is preserved: every point of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) that was covered by \\(L_c\\) is still covered (by \\(S_c\\)), and every other point remains covered by the unchanged lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive construc