e one of the non‑sunny lines \\(L_t\\) without losing coverage of any lattice point.\n\n*Step 4 – Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  All are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n*Step 5 – Construction for \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\) with \\(1\\le c_i\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(c_i\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=c_i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is sunny (its slope is undefined).  The point \\((c_i,\\,n+1-c_i)\\) that lies on \\(V_i\\) is precisely the point that previously belonged to the line \\(L_{\\,n+1-c_i}\\).  Consequently we may delete the line \\(L_{\\,n+1-c_i}\\) from the family \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) vertical sunny lines.  The resulting collection consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every lattice point, and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  This works for any \\(k\\ge 2\\) because the indices \\(n+1-c_i\\) are distinct, guaranteeing that the deleted non‑sunny lines are distinct.\n\n*Step 6 – Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers the lattice and contains exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).  Remove \\(S\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are all non‑sunny, i.e. each is parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Because the lattice contains points with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, a horizontal line can cover at most one point of the lattice; similarly a vertical line can cover at most one point.  Hence among the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines at most two can be horizontal/vertical, the rest must be of slope \\(-1\\).  Any line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one lattice point, because two distinct points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a+b=a'+b'\\) would give the same value of \\(a+b\\) and thus lie on the same line \\(L_t\\); but the lattice contains exactly one point for each value of \\(a+b\\).  Consequently each non‑sunny line covers at most one lattice point, so the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points.  The sunny line \\(S\\) can cover at most two lattice points (a line not parallel to the three forbidden directions can intersect the triangular lattice in at most two points, because any three collinear lattice points would force the line to have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Hence the whole family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) can cover at most \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) points, whereas the lattice contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n+1\\) points for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  This contradiction shows that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0\\) and all integers from \\(2\\) up to \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) consists of the points of a right‑isosceles triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A family of non‑sunny covering lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) with \\(t=a+b-1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  Each \\(L_t\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing non‑sunny lines