on, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and containing exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{N}=\\mathcal{F}\\setminus\\{S\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines; each line in \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) is non‑sunny, i.e. it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Horizontal or vertical lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can intersect \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) only at the point \\((n+1-c,c)\\); similarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) only at \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  Hence each horizontal or vertical line covers at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nIf two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), then \\(a+b=a'+b'\\).  But for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(t\\).  Consequently a line of slope \\(-1\\) can also contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nThus **every** line in \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) covers at most one lattice point, so the whole set \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nNow consider the sunny line \\(S\\).  Since \\(S\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it cannot contain three collinear points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\); otherwise the line would have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(S\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nTherefore the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+2=n+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n+1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThis contradiction shows that a covering family with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\