            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is a right‑isosceles triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A non‑sunny covering family  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) with \\(t=a+b-1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Each \\(L_t\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  Hence the case \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nFix a point \\((c,n+1-c)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y-\\bigl(n+1-c\\bigr)=\\sqrt2\\bigl(x-c\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) is irrational, therefore \\(S_c\\) contains **no other** integer point; in particular it meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) exactly at \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \nThe point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) also lies on the non‑sunny line \\(L_{\\,n+1-c}\\) from (6).  Consequently we may delete \\(L_{\\,n+1-c}\\) from a covering family and insert \\(S_c\\) without losing any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nSince \\(\\sqrt2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(S_c\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nc_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nForm the sunny lines \\(S_{c_i}\\) as in (8) and delete the corresponding non‑sunny lines \\(L_{\\,n+1-c_i}\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\Bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_{\\,n+1-c_i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigr)\n\\cup\\{S_{c_i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (by the argument of §2), and contains precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{c_i}\\).  Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) such a family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of a single sunny line  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{S\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nso \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-1\\) and every line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Horizontal or vertical lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) only at \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets it only at \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  Hence each such line covers at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=t+1\\) for some integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  For each such \\(t\\) there is exactly one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfying the equation, namely \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\).  Consequently every line of slope \\(-1\\) also covers at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nTherefore the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nNow consider the sunny line \\(S\\).  Because its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) cannot be collinear on \\(S\\): three collinear lattice points would force the line to be horizontal, vertical, or have slope \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(S\\) can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThus the whole family \\(\\mathcal G\\) can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+2=n+1\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  But by (5) we have \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  This contradiction shows that a covering family with exactly one sunny line cannot exist; the value \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
