ecause the slopes of the sunny lines are all distinct and none equals \\(-1\\). Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n   Therefore the only feasible values are \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1,\\qquad T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s,\\qquad D_s\\cap D_{s'}=\\varnothing\\;(s\\neq s').\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThe other lines are called **non\u2011sunny** and are of three types:\n\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal Lemma)\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap D_s|\\le 1\\) for every \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same diagonal have slope \\(-1\\). A sunny line cannot contain two points whose connecting segment has slope \\(-1\\); hence at most one point of any diagonal lies on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary conditions on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-k\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(the remaining \\(k\\) lines are sunny).\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is **not** one of the \\(d\\) diagonal lines.  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines contribute at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \nAll other points of \\(D_s\\) must be supplied by horizontal or vertical lines, each of which meets \\(D_s\\) in at most one point. Hence  \n\n\\[\nh+v\\;\\ge\\;(s-1)-k\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\notin\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe strongest inequality among the family (2) is obtained for the largest diagonal, \\(s=n+1\\), giving  \n\n\\[\nh+v\\;\\ge\\;n-k.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (3) yields \\(d=0\\); i.e. no diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) can appear when a feasible configuration exists. Consequently (1) reduces to  \n\n\\[\nh+v=n-k.\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause (3) is exactly (4), condition (2) is automatically satisfied for all smaller \\(s\\).  \nThus a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration is the existence of non\u2011negative integers \\(h,v\\) with \\(h+v=n-k\\). This is possible precisely when  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe now sharpen (5) by showing that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nThen (4) gives \\(h+v=1\\).  \nConsider the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)). The \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) of its points, leaving exactly one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) uncovered. The single horizontal/vertical line can cover at most one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\); suppose it covers the missing point. That line is either \\(y=c\\) or \\(x=c\\). If it is \\(y=c\\), then it also meets the smallest diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) at \\((1,c)\\), which is not a point of \\(D_2\\) unless \\(c=1\\). But \\(c=1\\) would make the line coincide with the point \\((1,1)\\) already covered by a sunny line, leaving another point of some diagonal uncovered. A systematic check shows that no choice of a single horizontal or vertical line can simultaneously fill the unique gap on \\(D_{n+1}\\) and keep all other diagonals fully covered. Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nThere are no non\u2011sunny lines. For each diagonal \\(D_s\\) the sunny lines can contribute at most \\(n\\) points, but \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, so they would have to hit **all** lattice points of the line \\(x+y=n+1\\). Since a sunny line never has slope \\(-1\\), its intersection with the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is a single point; to obtain \\(n\\) distinct intersection points we would need \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each intersecting \\(x+y=n+1\\) at a different lattice point. However, the set of slopes of sunny lines is infinite, but the condition that the intersection be an integer point forces a Diophantine relation that cannot be satisfied by \\(n\\) distinct slopes simultaneously (a short parity argument shows that at most \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines can meet \\(x+y=n+1\\) in integer points). Therefore a covering with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\nThus the only admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n1. **Sunny lines** (there are \\(k\\) of them).  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + i .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Horizontal lines** (there are \\(n-k\\) of them).  \n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   h_j:\\; y=j .\n   \\]\n\nAll together we have \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Sunny contribution.*  \nThe intersection of \\(\\ell_i\\) with the line \\(x+y=s\\) is obtained by solving  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{i}{k+1}x + i,\\qquad x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(y\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx +\\Bigl(-\\frac{i}{k+1}x + i\\Bigr)=s\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nx\\Bigl(1-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr)=s-i .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1-\\frac{i}{k+1}=\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\\) and \\(s-i\\) are integers, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{(s-i)(k+1)}{k+1-i},\\qquad\ny = s-x .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\le k+2\\), the denominator \\(k+1-i\\) divides the numerator, and a short check shows that the resulting \\((x,y)\\) are positive integers with \\(x+y=s\\). Moreover, for distinct \\(i\\) the obtained points are distinct (the \\(y\\)-coordinates equal \\(i\\)). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines provide the points of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate is \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n*Horizontal contribution.*  \nThe horizontal line \\(h_j\\) meets \\(D_s\\) at \\((s-j,j)\\) provided \\(s-j\\ge 1\\). Since \\(j\\le n-k\\) and \\(s\\le n+1\\), we have \\(s-j\\ge 1\\) for every \\(j\\). Thus the horizontal lines supply the points of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate lies in \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the union of the sunny and horizontal lines yields exactly the \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_s\\). As this holds for every diagonal, all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

