n exist for **any** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); no further restriction appears.\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 Sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\) with \\(1\\le t_i\\le n\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + t_i .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   Use the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   h_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\]\n\n   (If one prefers vertical lines, the same argument works with \\(x=j\\).)\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 Verification of coverage.*  \n   Fix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n   \u2013 The horizontal line \\(h_j\\) meets \\(D_s\\) at the point \\((s-j,j)\\) provided \\(1\\le j\\le m\\) and \\(s-j\\ge 1\\).  \n   Because \\(j\\le m=n-k\\) and \\(s\\le n+1\\), we indeed have \\(s-j\\ge 1\\).  \n   Hence the horizontals cover exactly the points of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\).\n\n   \u2013 For each sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) we solve  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{i}{k+1}x + t_i,\\qquad x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   Eliminating \\(y\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x\\Bigl(1-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr)=s-t_i\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   x = \\frac{(s-t_i)(k+1)}{k+1-i},\\qquad\n   y = s-x .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(1\\le t_i\\le n\\), the numerator \\((s-t_i)(k+1)\\) is an integer multiple of the denominator \\(k+1-i\\); consequently \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are integers.  \n   Moreover \\(x\\ge 1\\) and \\(y\\ge 1\\) because \\(s\\ge t_i+1\\) (the choice \\(t_i\\le n\\) guarantees this) and \\(s\\le n+1\\).  \n   Thus each \\(\\ell_i\\) contributes a point of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(t_i\\).\n\n   Because the set \\(\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\) is disjoint from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) (we simply take \\(t_i=m+i\\)), the points supplied by the sunny lines are exactly those of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate lies in \\(\\{m+1,\\dots ,m+k\\}=\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(D_s\\) (its second coordinate runs from \\(1\\) to \\(s-1\\)) is covered: the first \\(m\\) of them by the horizontals, the next \\(k\\) of them by the sunny lines, and if \\(s-1>m+k\\) (which can happen only when \\(s=n+1\\)) the remaining points are also supplied by the sunny lines because we have taken \\(t_i\\) up to \\(n\\). In all cases the union of the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(m\\) horizontals equals the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n   Since this holds for every diagonal, all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered. The total number of lines is \\(k+m=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n6. **Impossibility for other \\(k\\).**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   If \\(k>n\\) we would need more than \\(n\\) lines, contradicting the statement of the problem.  \n   Hence no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n**Conclusion** \u2013 the set of admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

