nals \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge m+i+1\\); for such \\(s\\) we have \\(0\\le s-(m+i)\\le k\\). … Because \\(d=k+1+i>k\\), the only multiple of \\(d\\) lying in the interval \\([0,k]\\) is \\(0\\). Therefore \\(d\\mid (s-m-i)\\) forces \\(s-m-i=0\\), i.e. \\(s=m+i\\). … Consequently the only diagonals on which \\(\\ell_i\\) actually meets a lattice point of \\(T_n\\) are those with \\(s>m+i\\); for those diagonals the fraction in (2) simplifies to an integer because the denominator divides the product …”\n\n**Critical Error:**  \nThe argument is contradictory:\n\n* First it deduces that the only way \\(d\\mid (s-m-i)\\) can hold is when \\(s-m-i=0\\).  \n* Then it claims that for \\(s>m+i\\) the fraction still becomes an integer because “the denominator divides the product by construction of the line”. This is precisely the unjustified claim identified earlier.  \n\nNo construction guarantees that \\(d\\) divides \\((s-m-i)(k+1)\\) when \\(s-m-i\\neq 0\\). The “by construction” statement is false; the lines were defined without any reference to such a divisibility condition. Hence the whole verification step is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Impossibility for other \\(k\\) (Step 7)\n\n> “The necessary argument in step 4 shows that any configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). The explicit construction in step 5 shows that every integer in this interval is attainable.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe necessity part is correct, but the sufficiency part relies on the flawed construction of Step 5. Since the construction does not actually cover all required points, the claim that every \\(k\\) in \\([0,n]\\) is attainable is **unproved**.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe solution correctly establishes the necessary bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), but the claimed construction for all admissible \\(k\\) fails because the “sunny” lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are not guaranteed to pass through the required integer lattice points. The core divisibility argument is incorrect, leading to uncovered po