nct, we cannot use the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) together with any other line that also meets \\(D_{n+1}\\); otherwise we would obtain fewer than \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n3. **Implication for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct points on \\(D_{n+1}\\) we must use **\\(n-k\\) horizontal (or vertical) lines**, each contributing a different point, and the \\(k\\) sunny lines must each contribute a different point as well.  \n   This forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); no further restriction follows from the diagonal alone.\n\n4. **Why the previously advertised construction fails for \\(k>0\\).**  \n   The construction proposed the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+(m+i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   where \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   Solving \\(\\ell_i\\) together with the equation of a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{(s-m-i)(k+1)}{k+1+i},\\qquad\n   y=s-x .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The claim that the right‑hand side of (2) is always an integer is **false**.  \n   The denominator \\(k+1+i\\) does **not** in general divide the numerator \\((s-m-i)(k+1)\\); the argument that “\\(\\gcd(k+1+i,k+1)=1\\) forces the denominator to divide \\(s-m-i\\)” is incorrect because there is no reason for \\(k+1+i\\) to divide \\(s-m-i\\).  \n\n   A concrete counter‑example is obtained with \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\) (hence \\(m=2\\)).  \n   The single sunny line is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=\\frac{1}{2}x+3 .\n   \\]\n\n   For the diagonal \\(D_4\\) (\\(s=4\\)) equation (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{(4-2-1)\\cdot2}{2+1}= \\frac{2}{3},\n   \\]\n\n   which is not an integer; consequently \\(\\ell_1\\) does **not** pass through any lattice point of \\(D_4\\).  \n   Hence the point \\((1,3)\\in D_4\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the required covering property.\n\n   Similar failures occur for all \\(k\\ge 1\\); therefore the construction does not establish the existence of configurations with \\(k>0\\).\n\