vers all points \\((1,b)\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)), the horizontal line covers all points \\((a,1)\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \n   *The only points of the required set not yet covered are those with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such points, namely \\((2,2), (3,2),\\dots ,(n,2)\\) together with \\((2,3),\\dots ,(n, n-1)\\).  \n   *For each integer \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) draw the sunny line \\(x+y=c\\). This line is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\) only when \\(c=0\\), so for \\(c\\ge 3\\) it is sunny. The line \\(x+y=c\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\); in particular it covers the required points whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).  \n   *Thus we use the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(x+y=3, x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n\\). All \\(n\\) lines are distinct and the covering condition is satisfied. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion** –  \n   *The counting argument of step 2 shows that fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are impossible.  \n   *Steps 3–5 give explicit constructions for \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a non‑sunny line has one of the forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFix a direction among the three in (2).  \nA line of that direction can intersect \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points:\n\n* for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and the admissible\n  points are \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) of them;\n* the same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\);\n* for a line \\(x+y=c\\) we need \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible points are\n  \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence **any** collection of non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(3n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the three families of non‑sunny lines together cannot cover all of\n\\(S_n\\).  Consequently at least one of the \\(n\\) required lines must be sunny.\n\nAssume that exactly two lines are non‑sunny.  Removing those two lines leaves\n\\(n-2\\) lines that must cover the points of \\(S_n\\) not already covered.\nThe same counting argument applied to the remaining points shows that a third\nnon‑sunny line would be necessary, contradicting the assumption that only two\nare non‑sunny.  Hence **at most two** of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{at least }n-2\\text{ of the lines must be sunny}.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit configurations with exactly \\(n\\), \\(n-1\\) and \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\; \\text{through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(a,n+2-a).\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the second point is not required\nbut its presence guarantees that the slope of \\(L_a\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_a=\\frac{n+1-a}{a-1}\\quad(\\text{with the convention }m_1=\\infty).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_a\\) is neither horizontal (\\(m=0\\)), nor vertical (\\(m=\\infty\\)),\nnor of slope \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_a\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) are distinct because they have distinct\nslopes.  \n\nEvery required point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); hence it lies\non the line \\(L_a\\).  Therefore the family \\(\\{L_a\\}\\) covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) draw the sunny line \\(L_a\\) defined in the\nprevious subsection.  The line \\(L_a\\) contains the point \\((a,1)\\) and\ntherefore also every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{V\\}\\cup\\{L_a\\mid a=2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(V\\)) is non‑sunny,\nand it covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nChoose the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(H:y=1\\).\nThey cover respectively all points with first coordinate \\(1\\) and all points\nwith second coordinate \\(1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\neq 0\\), the line \\(D_c\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is\nsunny.  The line \\(D_c\\) contains precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) whose\ncoordinates sum to \\(c\\).  As \\(c\\) runs from \\(3\\) to \\(n\\), every remaining\npoint of \\(S_n\\) (those with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\)) is covered exactly once.\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{V,\\,H\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid c=3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which (\\(V\\) and \\(H\\)) are\nnon‑sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (4) we know that any admissible configuration must have at least\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Sections 2.1–2.3 provide explicit configurations that\nrealise the three possibilities \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  Hence no\nother value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n.}\n\\]"
