s together cannot cover all of\n\\(S_n\\); at least one of the required \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.\n\nAssume that two lines are non‑sunny.  Removing them leaves \\(n-2\\) lines that\nmust cover the points of \\(S_n\\) not already covered.  Applying the same\ncounting argument to the uncovered points shows that a third non‑sunny line\nwould be necessary, contradicting the assumption that only two are non‑sunny.\nConsequently **at most two** of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible configuration contains at least }n-2\\text{ sunny lines}.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_a:=\\text{the line through }P_a=(a,1)\\text{ and }Q_a=(a+1,n+1-a).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_a\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_a=\\frac{n-a}{a},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\) (because \\(n>a\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (because \\(a\\neq0\\)),\nand never \\(-1\\) (since \\(\\frac{n-a}{a}=-1\\) would give \\(n=0\\)).  \nHence every \\(L_a\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have distinct\nslopes.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the three points\n\\(P_a=(a,1),\\;(a,b),\\;Q_a=(a+1,n+1-a)\\) are collinear; consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) covers all of\n\\(S_n\\) and consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) use the sunny line \\(L_a\\) defined above.\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\) as shown in\nthe previous subsection.  Hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{V\\}\\cup\\{L_a\\mid a=2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\); exactly one line (\\(V\\)) is non‑sunny, the remaining \\(n-1\\) are\nsunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nV:x=1,\\qquad H:y=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) and \\(H\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) consider  \n\n\\[\nD_c:x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\neq0\\), each \\(D_c\\) is sunny.  A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with\n\\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(D_c\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{V,H\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which (\\(V\\) and \\(H\\)) are\nnon‑sunny, and it covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nEquation (4) shows that any admissible configuration must have at least\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Sections 2.1–2.3 provide explicit configurations with\n\\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  Therefore no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
