oint.  The resulting \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(S'\\);\n   the uncovered point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny.  Hence a configuration with exactly\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Construction with all \\(n\\) lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).**  \n   Number the diagonals of \\(S\\) by their sums:\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The size of \\(D_s\\) is \\(s-1\\) and the largest diagonal has size \\(n\\).\n\n   We shall produce \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that\n   each line meets every diagonal in exactly one lattice point.\n   Consequently each diagonal contributes \\(n\\) points, i.e. the total\n   number of incidences is \\(n\\cdot n\\ge |S|\\); therefore the\n   \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   **Choice of slopes.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) set the slope\n   \\[\n   m_i = i\\qquad(\\text{so }m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}).\n   \\]\n   All these slopes are distinct and finite, hence each corresponding\n   line will be sunny.\n\n   **Determination of intercepts.**  \n   For a fixed slope \\(m_i\\) we require that its intersection with the\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\) be a lattice point.  The intersection of the line\n   \\(y=m_i x +c_i\\) with the line \\(x+y=s\\) is obtained by solving\n   \\[\n   x + (m_i x +c_i)=s\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   x=\\frac{s-c_i}{m_i+1},\\qquad\n   y=s-x .\n   \\]\n   Hence we need \\(s-c_i\\) to be divisible by \\(m_i+1\\) for every\n   \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n   Choose the intercept\n   \\[\n   c_i = i\\;( \\bmod\\; i+1),\\qquad 0\\le c_i\\le i .\n   \\]\n   More concretely, set \\(c_i = i\\).  Then for any \\(s\\) we have\n   \\[\n   s-c_i = s-i .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(m_i+1 = i+1\\), the integer division\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s-i}{i+1}\n   \\]\n   is an integer exactly when \\(s\\equiv i\\pmod{i+1}\\).  To obtain an\n   integer for **every** \\(s\\) we modify the intercept by adding a\n   suitable multiple of \\(i+1\\).  Define\n   \\[\n   c_i = i - (i+1)\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor = i .\n   \\]\n   Then for each \\(s\\) write \\(s = q(i+1)+r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le i\\).\n   Taking \\(c_i = r\\) gives\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{s-r}{i+1}=q\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1},\n   \\qquad\n   y=s-x = q i + r .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(1\\le q\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le r\\le i\\), both coordinates are\n   positive integers and satisfy \\(x+y=s\\le n+1\\).  Thus for each\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\) the line of slope \\(m_i\\) with intercept\n   \\(c_i=r\\) (where \\(r\\equiv s\\pmod{i+1}\\) and \\(0\\le r\\le i\\)) passes\n   through a lattice point of that diagonal.\n\n   Performing this construction for every \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) yields\n   \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (the slopes are distinct, and\n   the intercepts are chosen from different residue classes, so the\n   lines are distinct).  By the argument above each diagonal \\(D_s\\)\n   contains exactly one point of each \\(\\ell_i\\); consequently every\n   point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   Hence a configuration with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny exists for every\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining the constructions of items 2–5 we have exhibited, for each\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\ncovering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_s\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence\nthey are non‑sunny.  If \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique\n\\(s\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover \\(S\\) and contain no sunny line.\nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Keep the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S\\) whose sum does not exceed\n\\(n-k+1\\).\n\nThe remaining points lie on the \\(k\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad\ns=n-k+2,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each diagonal \\(D_s\\) choose a distinct sunny line that passes\nthrough one still‑uncovered lattice point of \\(D_s\\) (any slope different\nfrom \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) will do).  Because a sunny line meets a given\ndiagonal in at most one point, the \\(k\\) newly added lines are distinct.\nTogether with the \\(n-k\\) lines in (2) they cover every point of \\(S\\).\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nRemove the point \\((1,1)\\) from \\(S\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nS' = S\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\},\n\\qquad \n|S'| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1 \\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_{n-1}\\) (for\ninstance \\(m_i=i\\)).  Proceed inductively: at step \\(i\\) pick any point\nof \\(S'\\) not used before, say \\((a_i,b_i)\\), and define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\); the slopes are\ndifferent, so the lines are distinct.  After \\(n-1\\) steps all points of\n\\(S'\\) are covered.  The remaining point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the\nvertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny.  Hence a configuration with\nexactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe construct \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that each\ndiagonal \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) contains exactly one point of each\n\\(\\ell_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one\n\\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n**Slopes.**  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nm_i = i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(m_i\\) are distinct and satisfy \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); therefore\neach line with slope \\(m_i\\) is sunny.\n\n**Intercepts.**  For a fixed \\(i\\) and a diagonal \\(D_s\\) we need the\nintersection of the line \\(y=m_i x +c_i\\) with the line \\(x+y=s\\) to be\na lattice point.  Solving  \n\n\\[\nx+(m_i x +c_i)=s\n\\Longrightarrow\nx=\\frac{s-c_i}{m_i+1},\\qquad\ny=s-x .\n\\]\n\nThus we require \\(s-c_i\\) to be divisible by \\(m_i+1=i+1\\) for every\n\\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nWrite each \\(s\\) uniquely as  \n\n\\[\ns = q(i+1)+r,\\qquad 0\\le r\\le i .\n\\]\n\nChoose the intercept  \n\n\\[\nc_i = r .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s-r}{i+1}=q\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1},\n\\qquad\ny=s-x = q i + r .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le q\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le r\\le i\\), both coordinates are\npositive integers and satisfy \\(x+y=s\\le n+1\\); hence \\((x,y)\\in D_s\\).\n\nThus for each diagonal \\(D_s\\) the line of slope \\(m_i\\) with intercept\n\\(c_i=r\\) (the remainder of \\(s\\) modulo \\(i+1\\)) passes through a\nlattice point of that diagonal.  Performing this for every\n\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) yields \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_i\\).  By construction each diagonal contains exactly one point of\neach \\(\\ell_i\\); consequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one\n\\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nHence a configuration with **all \\(n\\) lines sunny** exists for every\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\nn\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Therefore the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
