 cover every point of \\(S\\).\n\n   Because the construction works for any choice of \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\), we have exhibited configurations for all such \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\), for \\(k=n\\), and for every intermediate \\(k\\) prove that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_s\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover \\(S\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x - (i-1) .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(i\\) is an integer \\(\\ge 1\\); therefore it is different from \\(0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nLet \\(D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) be the diagonal of sum \\(s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nIntersecting \\(\\ell_i\\) with the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) gives\n\n\\[\ny = i\\,x-(i-1),\\qquad x+y=s\n\\Longrightarrow\ns-x = i\\,x-(i-1)\n\\Longrightarrow\nx = \\frac{s+i-1}{i+1},\\qquad\ny = s-x .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) are integers, the numerator \\(s+i-1\\) is congruent to \\(s-1\\) modulo \\(i+1\\).  Write \\(s-1 = q(i+1)+r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le i\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{q(i+1)+r+i}{i+1}=q+1,\\qquad\ny = s-x = (q+1)i + r .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are positive integers and satisfy \\(x+y=s\\); consequently the intersection point belongs to \\(D_s\\).  As \\(s\\) runs through \\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\), the pair \\((q,r)\\) runs through all admissible integer pairs, so **every** point of every diagonal appears as the intersection of some \\(\\ell_i\\) with that diagonal.  Hence each point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1 .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together they cover all points of \\(S\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(n-k+1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each remaining diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=n-k+2,\\dots ,n+1 ,\n\\]\n\nchoose the point \\((1,s-1)\\in D_s\\) and define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y = (s-1)x-(s-2) .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_s\\) equals \\(s-1\\ge n-k+1\\ge 2\\); therefore it is different from \\(0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny.  \n\nSubstituting the diagonal equation \\(x+y=s\\) into (3.2) gives\n\n\\[\ns-x = (s-1)x-(s-2)\n\\Longrightarrow\ns = s x\n\\Longrightarrow\nx=1,\n\\]\n\nand then \\(y=s-1\\).  Hence **every** point of the diagonal \\(D_s\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_s\\); indeed the whole diagonal consists of the points \\((1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\), and each of them fulfills \\(y=(s-1)x-(s-2)\\).  Consequently the line \\(\\ell_s\\) contains the entire diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_s\\) are distinct because they have different slopes (\\(s-1\\) is different for different \\(s\\)).  All of them are sunny.\n\n#### 3.3  Coverage  \n\n- Points with \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\) are covered by the non‑sunny lines (3.1).  \n- Points with \\(a+b=s\\ge n-k+2\\) lie on the diagonal \\(D_s\\) and, as shown above, the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_s\\) (3.2) contains the whole diagonal.  \n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them (\\(\\ell_s\\)) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final statement\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
