h coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(x_{i,s}+y_{i,s}=s\\); hence \\((x_{i,s},y_{i,s})\\in D_s\\).  \n   As \\(s\\) runs through \\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\) the pair \\((q,r)\\) runs through all admissible integer pairs, so **every** point of every diagonal appears as the intersection of some \\(\\ell_i\\) with that diagonal.  \n   Consequently each point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).\n\n   Thus the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Partial result for intermediate \\(k\\).**  \n   The idea is to keep \\(n-k\\) of the non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=s\\) (which cover the points whose sum is small) and to replace the remaining \\(k\\) diagonals by sunny lines.  \n   A na\u00efve attempt \u2013 taking for each remaining diagonal \\(D_s\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=(s-1)x-(s-2)\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   \u2013 fails because (4.1) contains only the single point \\((1,s-1)\\) of the diagonal, not the whole diagonal.  \n   More elaborate constructions are possible (for example, letting a sunny line pass through two points belonging to two different remaining diagonals), but a systematic method that works for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been found.  \n   Hence at present we can only assert the existence of configurations for the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_s\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny