\\(D_s\\) (\\(s=n-k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) by a sunny line.  \n\nThe line  \n\n\\[\ny=(s-1)x-(s-2)\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\npasses through the point \\((1,s-1)\\) of the diagonal, but a direct substitution shows that it does **not** contain the other points of the same diagonal (for instance, when \\(s=4\\) the diagonal points are \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) and only \\((1,3)\\) satisfies (3.2)).  \n\nConsequently the simple replacement described above does **not** yield a covering of all points of \\(S\\).  \nMore sophisticated arrangements\u2014e.g. letting a sunny line pass through two points belonging to two different remaining diagonals\u2014might work, but a uniform construction that succeeds for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been established here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved rigorously that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n* the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\));\n* the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable (the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (2.1)).\n\nThe existence of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains an open problem within the present work.  \n\nHence the set of values of \\(k\\) that are **known** to be realizable is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]"

