n‑sunny line can contain **only** points belonging to a single diagonal \\(D_s\\) (if its direction is slope \\(-1\\)) or can contain at most one point from each diagonal (if its direction is horizontal or vertical).  \n\n   *Counting points on the diagonals.*  \n   The diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points.  Hence the total number of points that lie on the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\cdot (n+1) \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (the worst case is when all non‑sunny lines are of slope \\(-1\\) and each coincides with a different diagonal; there are at most \\(n-k\\) such diagonals).\n\n   *Counting points that must be covered by sunny lines.*  \n   The whole set \\(S\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  Consequently the sunny lines have to cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)(n+1)=\\frac{(k-1)(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   *A sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point.*  \n   Indeed, if a sunny line met a diagonal \\(D_s\\) in two distinct points, its slope would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  Hence a single sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points altogether.  With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can therefore cover at most \\(kn\\) points.\n\n   Combining this upper bound with (5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   kn\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(k-1)(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (2k-n-1)(n+1)\\le 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\), the factor \\(2k-n-1\\) is **negative** only when \\(k\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  For any such \\(k\\) inequality (6) is satisfied, but we must also respect the fact that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-k)(n+1)\\) points, as noted in (3).  Substituting this maximal coverage into (5) we obtain the stricter requirement  \n\n   \\[\n   kn\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)(n+1)=\\frac{k(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2kn\\ge k(n+1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2n\\ge n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is true, but equality forces \\(k=n\\).  The only way to achieve the bound is when every non‑sunny line is of slope \\(-1\\) and coincides with a distinct diagonal, and every sunny line meets **every** diagonal exactly once.  The latter is impossible for \\(k<n\\): a sunny line meeting all \\(n\\) diagonals would have to contain \\(n\\) points with distinct sums, which forces its slope to be \\(-1\\) (the unique line intersecting each diagonal in a distinct point is precisely a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  Therefore a configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n   Consequently the only feasible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_s\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover \\(S\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(i\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nIntersect \\(\\ell_i\\) with a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nSolving  \n\n\\[\ny = i\\,x-(i-1),\\qquad x+y=s\n\\]\n\ngives  \n\n\\[\nx_{i,s}= \\frac{s+i-1}{i+1},\\qquad \ny_{i,s}= s-x_{i,s}= \\frac{i(s-1)}{i+1}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(s-1=(i+1)q+r\\) with integers \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(0\\le r\\le i\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nx_{i,s}=q+1,\\qquad y_{i,s}=iq+r .\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(x_{i,s}+y_{i,s}=s\\); therefore \\((x_{i,s},y_{i,s})\\in D_s\\).\n\nConversely, fix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) and a point \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b=s\\) we can write \\(a-1=q\\) and \\(b=iq+r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le i\\) for a unique \\(i\\) (namely \\(i=r\\) if \\(r>0\\), otherwise \\(i=1\\)).  Substituting this \\(i\\) into (2.2) reproduces \\((a,b)\\).  Hence **every** point of every diagonal appears as the intersection of some \\(\\ell_i\\) with that diagonal.\n\nConsequently each point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny; each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions: horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most one point from each diagonal \\(D_s\\), unless it is of slope \\(-1\\), in which case it coincides with a whole diagonal.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line meets a diagonal \\(D_s\\) in two distinct points, the two points have the same sum of coordinates, so the line’s slope must be \\(-1\\).  ∎\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{ns}}\\le (n-k)(n+1), \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nthe right‑hand side being attained when all non‑sunny lines are of slope \\(-1\\) and each coincides with a different diagonal (there are at most \\(n\\) diagonals).\n\nThe whole set \\(S\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Therefore the sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{s}}=|S|-P_{\\text{ns}}\\ge\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)(n+1)=\\frac{(k-1)(n+1)}{2} \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line met a diagonal in two points, its slope would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nConsequently a single sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (one from each diagonal), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(kn\\) points:\n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{s}}\\le kn . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.3) and (3.4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nkn\\ge\\frac{(k-1)(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (3.5) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2k-n-1)(n+1)\\le 0 .\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\), the factor \\(2k-n-1\\) is **negative** only when \\(k\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  For such \\(k\\) inequality (3.6) holds, but recall that equality in (3.1) is required for (3.3) to be tight.  Equality in (3.1) forces every non‑sunny line to be of slope \\(-1\\) and to coincide with a distinct diagonal; consequently the set of diagonals not covered by non‑sunny lines consists of exactly \\(k\\) diagonals.\n\nNow each sunny line must meet **all** of those \\(k\\) remaining diagonals (otherwise some point would remain uncovered).  By Lemma 2 a sunny line can meet each of the \\(k\\) diagonals in at most one point, so a sunny line meeting all \\(k\\) diagonals would contain exactly \\(k\\) points, one from each diagonal.  The only line that meets every diagonal of the family \\(\\{D_s\\mid s\\in I\\}\\) (where \\(I\\) is a set of \\(k\\) distinct sums) in distinct points is the line of slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\) for any constant \\(c\\) meets each diagonal in at most one point, and the only way to meet *all* of them is to be parallel to them, i.e. to have slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence any sunny line that meets all remaining diagonals would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting its sunniness.\n\nTherefore the assumption that a configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists leads to a contradiction.  No such configuration can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe problem admits configurations exactly for  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n .\n\\]\n\nBoth values are realizable by the explicit constructions given in Sections 1 and 2, and all intermediate values are impossible by the counting argument of Section 3.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
