as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n     If \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in D_{s}\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\), then  \n     \\[\n     a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}=s,\\qquad \n     b_{1}=ma_{1}+b,\\; b_{2}=ma_{2}+b .\n     \\]\n     Subtracting gives \\((1+m)(a_{1}-a_{2})=0\\).  \n     Since \\(m\\neq-1\\), we obtain \\(a_{1}=a_{2}\\) and consequently \\(b_{1}=b_{2}\\), contradicting distinctness.\n\n   * (ii) **Vertical line** \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \n     Points of a diagonal satisfy \\(a+b=s\\); two distinct points on the same vertical line would have the same \\(a=c\\) but different \\(b\\), giving different sums \\(c+b\\). Hence at most one point of a fixed diagonal can lie on a vertical line.\n\n   * (iii) **Horizontal line** \\(y=c\\) is excluded because a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n   In all admissible cases a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n4. **Counting points on the largest diagonal.**  \n\n   The diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line can contribute at most one of them, whereas a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. of the form \\(x+y=t\\)) contains **all** points of a diagonal.  \n\n   Suppose we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Let \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines; each of them is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).\n\n   *All points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) must be covered.*  \n   If none of the non‑sunny lines equals \\(x+y=n+1\\), then the \\(k\\) sunny lines could cover at most \\(k\\le n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – impossible.  \n   Hence **the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) must be present** among the non‑sunny lines.\n\n5. **How many diagonals may be omitted?**  \n\n   For any \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\), the diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) has \\(s-1\\) points.  \n   If the line \\(x+y=s\\) is omitted, those