4) is equivalent to\n\n   \\[\n   M\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(|M|=k\\) forces \\(M\\) to be any \\(k\\)-element subset of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n5. **Sufficiency – constructing families for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   M=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\quad(\\text{if }k=0\\text{ take }M=\\varnothing).\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Include the lines \\(L_{s}\\) for all \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus M\\).  \n   There are \\(n-|M|=n-k\\) such lines, and in particular \\(L_{n+1}\\) is present.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x + \\frac{i}{k+1-i}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i}=(i,\\,k+1-i)\\in D_{k+1},\n   \\]\n\n   and its slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Because the points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).**  \n     Its \\(k\\) points are exactly the points \\(P_{i}\\) covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\).\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k\\).**  \n     For a fixed \\(s\\) the set \\(D_{s}\\) consists of the points \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\).  \n     Solving the equation of \\(\\ell_{i}\\) for \\(x\\) gives a unique intersection point with each diagonal; because the slopes are all different, the intersections are distinct. Consequently each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) (\\(s\\le k\\)) receives exactly one point from each sunny line, and since there are \\(k\\ge s-1\\) sunny lines, all \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_{s}\\) are covered.\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(s\\ge k+2\\).**  \n     These diagonals are covered by the non‑sunny line \\(L_{s}\\) (which is present because \\(s\\notin M\\)).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of the constructed family. The family consists of \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, none would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); in particular the line \\(L_{n+1}\\) would be missing. As argued in step 3, the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains \\(n\\) points, and a sunny line can cover at most one of them, so at most \\(n-1\\) points could be covered – contradiction. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\,}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
