 that is not sunny is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   *Vertical* lines have equation \\(x=c\\) and contain the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus a vertical line can contain up to \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   *Horizontal* lines have equation \\(y=c\\) and contain the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); they are equally useful.  \n   *Lines of slope \\(-1\\)* are precisely the lines \\(x+y=s\\) for integer \\(s\\).  \n   No other non‑sunny line can contain a point of \\(S\\) because any line with a different slope is sunny by definition.\n\n   Hence every non‑sunny line in a feasible configuration is either\n   \\[\n   V_{c}:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad H_{c}:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad\n   L_{s}:x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n   \\]\n\n4. **A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Consider the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\), which has \\(n\\) points.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   Consequently at least \\(n-(n-k)=k\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \n   Since a line of the form \\(L_{n+1}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains **all** points of \\(D_{n+1}\\), the only way to cover the remaining \\(n-k\\) points with non‑sunny lines is to include the line \\(L_{n+1}\\) among the non‑sunny ones.  \n   This shows that **\\(L_{n+1}\\) must be present** in every admissible family, but it does **not** impose any restriction on \\(k\\); the line \\(L_{n+1}\\) can be one of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   The only universal restriction comes from the fact that a sunny line can never be parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore the total number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sufficiency – construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Step 5.1 – non‑sunny part.*  \n   Include the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which covers the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   To obtain the remaining \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines we use vertical lines\n\n   \\[\n   V_{c}:x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\n   \\]\n\n   (if \\(n-k-1=0\\) we take none).  \n   These vertical lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and together with \\(L_{n+1}\\) give exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 5.2 – sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x + \\frac{i}{k+1-i}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (2) gives \\(y=k+1-i\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i}=(i,\\;k+1-i)\\in D_{k+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – verification that every point of \\(S\\) is covered.*\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).**  \n     By (3) each point of \\(D_{k+1}\\) is exactly one of the points \\(P_{i}\\) and is therefore covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\).\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k\\).**  \n     For a fixed \\(s\\) the set \\(D_{s}\\) consists of the points \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\).  \n     Solving (2) for \\(x\\) gives a unique intersection of \\(\\ell_{i}\\) with each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\); because the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\) are all distinct, the intersections are distinct for different \\(i\\).  \n     Since there are \\(k\\ge s-1\\) sunny lines, we can assign a different sunny line to each of the \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_{s}\\). Explicitly, the point \\((a,s-a)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{i}\\) where \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(a\\le s-1\\le k\\)). Hence every point of \\(D_{s}\\) is covered by a sunny line.\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n\\).**  \n     For such \\(s\\) the line \\(L_{s}:x+y=s\\) is not among the omitted diagonals (the only omitted diagonal is \\(L_{n+1}\\), which we already have). Consequently \\(L_{s}\\) is present among the vertical lines \\(V_{c}\\) together with \\(L_{n+1}\\): indeed each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a> n-k-1\\). In the latter case we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1\\); but then \\(b\\le k+1\\) and the point belongs to a diagonal already covered by the sunny lines (as shown above). Therefore every point of \\(D_{s}\\) is covered either by a vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\)) or by a sunny line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds that bound).\n\n   - **Diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).**  \n     All its points lie on the line \\(L_{n+1}\\), which is part of the construction.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   *Step 5.4 – count of sunny lines.*  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) from (2); all other lines are vertical or of slope \\(-1\\) and therefore non‑sunny. Thus the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n6. **Impossibility of values outside the interval.**  \n\n   By definition a family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot be negative nor exceed \\(n\\). Hence no integer \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   The set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n   In other words, **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is possible, and no other value is.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
